<!--
  Review Problems #2.

  Everything from the Chapter 3 worksheets (three-dimensional coordinates,
  vectors, the dot product, the cross product, parametrization of plane
  curves, lines and planes, conic sections and quadric surfaces) that the
  instructor's markup marked R2 rather than assigning to Assignments 4-6.

  Starred problems (ex-cross-circular-motion, the three projectile exercises
  and exer-parabolic-antenna) are optional and will not be examined.

  ex-3d-parabolic-cylinder and ex-3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid came from the
  three-dimensional coordinates worksheet; the markup asks for them to sit
  with the quadric surfaces problems, so they are last.

  rs-multiselect-quadrics ("Which of These Are Hyperboloids?") was moved here
  from the Interactive Problem Set (exercises-runestone-samples.ptx), where it
  filled the multiple-select slot.  That worksheet has since been dissolved,
  and its three Chapter 3 problems (rs-mc-vector-identity,
  rs-fillin-skew-lines and rs-parsons-no-work) close this file; its Chapter 4
  problems went to review-problems-3.ptx.

  Those Runestone problems are DEV-schema elements, so validate this file
  with the CLI's dev schema option; the stable schema reports false positives
  on them.  See CLAUDE.md for which markup triggers which question type.

  exercise-quadric-graph-match was marked R2 but has since been moved to
  Assignment 6 at the instructor's request.

  Notation: |v| has become \|v\| for the length of a vector throughout, and
  m2b-1a, m2b-1b and m2b-1c now use \vec u and \vec v in place of \vec A and
  \vec B.  pp-10 explains what collinear means, and pp-19 gains a part asking
  for the xy-projection of the curve of intersection.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-review-problems-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Review Problems #2</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These are the problems from the Chapter 3 topics that were not assigned in
Assignments 4-6, collected here for review. They are not assigned, but they
are fair game on an exam. Problems marked with a star (<m>*</m>) are the
exception: those are for the interested reader and will not be examined.
</p>

<p>
The topics they cover, with links to the notes:
</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li><p><xref ref="chapter-three-dimensional-coordinates" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="chapter-vectors" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-dot-product" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-cross-product" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-parametrization" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="chapter-lines-planes" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-conics" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-quadric-surfaces" text="title"/></p></li>
</ul>
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-describe-more">
<title>Describing More Equations and Inequalities Geometrically</title>
<statement>
<p>
Describe the geometrical meaning of the following equalities and
inequalities.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>y = -2</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>y = -2,\; z = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 + z^2 = 9</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 + z^2 \le 9,\; y \ge -1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>y^2 + z^2 = 4</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>y^2 + z^2 = 4,\; 0 \le x \le 5</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x = y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> This is the set of all points whose <m>y</m>-coordinate
equals <m>-2</m>, that is <m>\{(x,y,z) \in \R^3 : y = -2\}</m>. Both
<m>x</m> and <m>z</m> are unrestricted, so it is a plane parallel to the
<m>xz</m>-plane, sitting two units to the negative side of it, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-plane-y"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-plane-y">
<caption>
Part <alert>A</alert>: the plane <m>y=-2</m>, parallel to the
<m>xz</m>-plane.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded rectangle parallel to the x z plane, offset from it in the
negative y direction, representing the plane y equals negative two.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05, >=stealth]
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.75] (-1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,1.8) -- (-1.8,-2,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,1.8) -- (-1.8,-2,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (-2.2,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.8,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.2,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,-2.2);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.2) node[above] {$z$};
\node[cyan!70!black] at (-1.8,-2,1.5) {$y=-2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>B.</alert> This is the intersection of the two planes
<m>y = -2</m> and <m>z = 1</m>, which is a line. Only <m>x</m> is
unrestricted, so the line is parallel to the <m>x</m>-axis and passes
through the point <m>(0,-2,1)</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-line-y-z"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-line-y-z">
<caption>
Part <alert>B</alert>: the planes <m>y=-2</m> and <m>z=1</m> meet in a
line parallel to the <m>x</m>-axis, highlighted in red.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
<p>
Two shaded planes, one for y equals negative two and one for z equals
one, crossing each other. Their intersection is a red line parallel to
the x axis.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05, >=stealth]
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.55] (-1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,1.8) -- (-1.8,-2,1.8) -- cycle;
\fill[orange!25, opacity=0.55] (-1.8,-2.6,1) -- (1.8,-2.6,1) -- (1.8,2.2,1) -- (-1.8,2.2,1) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,-1.8) -- (1.8,-2,1.8) -- (-1.8,-2,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[orange!70!black] (-1.8,-2.6,1) -- (1.8,-2.6,1) -- (1.8,2.2,1) -- (-1.8,2.2,1) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (-2.2,0,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.8,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.2,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0,-2.2) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.2) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[red!70!black, very thick] (-1.8,-2,1) -- (1.8,-2,1);
\node[cyan!70!black, anchor=east] at (-1.9,-2,-1.85) {$y=-2$};
\node[orange!80!black, anchor=west] at (1.9,2.2,1) {$z=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>C.</alert> Comparing with the standard equation of a sphere, this
is the sphere of radius <m>3</m> centered at <m>(2,-1,0)</m>. Note the
center lies in the <m>xy</m>-plane, and since the radius is <m>3</m> the
sphere reaches from <m>z = -3</m> up to <m>z = 3</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-sphere-off"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-sphere-off">
<caption>
Part <alert>C</alert>: the sphere <m>(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2+z^2=9</m>, of
radius <m>3</m> centered at <m>(2,-1,0)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="45%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded sphere of radius three, offset from the origin toward the
point two, negative one, zero, with a dotted segment from the origin
to its center and a dashed ellipse marking its equator.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
\draw[gray, densely dotted] (0,0) -- (-2.78,-0.58);
\fill[gray] (-2.78,-0.58) circle (1pt);
\shade[ball color=cyan!30, opacity=0.92] (-2.78,-0.58) circle (1.55cm);
\draw[cyan!55!black, dashed, opacity=0.8] (-2.78,-0.58) ellipse (1.55cm and 0.43cm);
\node[gray!70!black, below=2pt] at (-2.78,-0.58) {$(2,-1,0)$};
% axes drawn last, so they stay visible where they cross the sphere;
% the x axis is shortened so its arrowhead and label sit in front of
% the sphere rather than buried in its middle
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.6) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (3.4,-0.9) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-1.5,-0.69) node[below left=1pt] {$x$};
\node[above right] at (0,0.05) {$0$};
\node[below] at (-2.78,-2.5) {$(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2+z^2=9$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>D.</alert> Replacing the equality by <m>\le</m> gives the solid
ball: every point whose distance from <m>(2,-1,0)</m> is at most
<m>3</m>. The extra condition <m>y \ge -1</m> keeps only the points on
one side of the plane <m>y = -1</m>. That plane passes through the
<em>center</em> of the ball, so exactly half of the ball survives: the
region is a solid half-ball of radius <m>3</m>, whose flat face is the
disk of radius <m>3</m> cut out of the plane <m>y = -1</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-halfball-off"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-halfball-off">
<caption>
Part <alert>D</alert>: the half-ball of radius <m>3</m> centered at
<m>(2,-1,0)</m>, cut by the condition <m>y \ge -1</m>, a plane through
its center.
</caption>
<image width="45%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded hemisphere sitting above a flat disk face at the plane y equals
negative one, offset from the origin toward the point two, negative one,
zero, with a dotted segment from the origin to the center of the disk.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05, >=stealth]
% the flat reference plane y=-1, exactly like part A's plane, so the
% orientation is guaranteed consistent
\fill[gray!15, opacity=0.7] (-2,-1,-3.7) -- (6,-1,-3.7) -- (6,-1,3.7) -- (-2,-1,3.7) -- cycle;
\draw[gray!60, thin] (-2,-1,-3.7) -- (6,-1,-3.7) -- (6,-1,3.7) -- (-2,-1,3.7) -- cycle;
% the curved dome (y from -1 up to the pole at y=2): stacked latitude
% circles of shrinking radius sqrt(9-(y+1)^2), each plotted directly in
% the true 3D coordinate system, giving a rounded, shaded cap
\foreach \y in {-0.85,-0.7,...,1.9} {
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\r}{sqrt(9-(\y+1)*(\y+1))}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\shd}{25+45*(\y+1)/3}
  \fill[cyan!\shd, opacity=0.85]
    plot[domain=0:360, samples=48, smooth cycle] ({2+\r*cos(\x)}, \y, {\r*sin(\x)});
}
% the flat cut disk (intersection with y=-1), same color family as the
% dome so the whole solid reads as one continuous surface
\fill[cyan!65, opacity=0.9]
  plot[domain=0:360, samples=72, smooth cycle] ({2+3*cos(\x)}, -1, {3*sin(\x)});
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick]
  plot[domain=0:360, samples=72, smooth cycle] ({2+3*cos(\x)}, -1, {3*sin(\x)});
% dotted line from the origin to the sphere's center
\draw[gray, densely dotted] (0,0,0) -- (2,-1,0);
\fill[gray] (2,-1,0) circle (1.3pt);
% axes drawn last, so they stay visible where they cross the shape
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (-2.2,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.4,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.8,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.4,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,-3.9);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,3.9) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above right] at (0,0,0) {$0$};
\node[gray!60!black] at (-1.6,-1,3.6) {$y=-1$};
\node[gray!70!black, below right=1pt] at (2,-1,0) {$(2,-1,0)$};
\node[cyan!55!black, below] at (2,-1,-4.2) {$(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2+z^2 \le 9,\; y \ge -1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>E.</alert> In the <m>yz</m>-plane, <m>y^2 + z^2 = 4</m> is a
circle of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin. In space <m>x</m> is
unrestricted, so the equation represents an infinitely long cylinder of
radius <m>2</m> whose axis is the <m>x</m>-axis. Had the missing
variable been <m>z</m> instead, the axis would have been the
<m>z</m>-axis: the axis of the cylinder is always the axis of the
variable that does not appear. The
left half of <xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-x"/> shows this cylinder.
</p>

<p>
<alert>F.</alert> The extra condition <m>0 \le x \le 5</m> cuts the
cylinder of part <alert>E</alert> down to a piece of length <m>5</m>,
running from the circle in the plane <m>x = 0</m> to the circle in the
plane <m>x = 5</m>, as on the right of
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-x"/>. It is a tube, not a solid: the two
end disks are not included, since the equation forces
<m>y^2 + z^2 = 4</m> exactly.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-x">
<caption>
Parts <alert>E</alert> and <alert>F</alert>. On the left, the infinite
cylinder <m>y^2+z^2=4</m>, whose axis is the <m>x</m>-axis; the dashed
lines indicate that it continues without bound in both directions. On
the right, the same cylinder cut down to <m>0 \le x \le 5</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
Two pictures side by side. On the left, a tube of radius two lying along
the x axis, with dashed lines continuing past both ends to show that it
is unbounded. On the right, the same tube cut off by the plane x equals
zero at one end and the plane x equals five at the other, leaving a
piece of length five with open ends.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.82, >=stealth]
% ------------- left: the infinite cylinder y^2+z^2=4 -------------
\begin{scope}
  % tube wall between the two drawn rims
  \fill[cyan!22, opacity=0.8] (-1.4,-1.5) rectangle (3.4,1.5);
  % both rim disks lighter than the wall: the tube is open, not capped,
  % so only the side wall gets the solid body color
  \fill[cyan!10, opacity=0.9] (-1.4,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \fill[cyan!10, opacity=0.9] (3.4,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-1.4,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (3.4,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.4,1.5) -- (3.4,1.5);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.4,-1.5) -- (3.4,-1.5);
  % dashed continuations: the cylinder is unbounded in both directions
  \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (-1.4,1.5) -- (-2.1,1.5);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (-1.4,-1.5) -- (-2.1,-1.5);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (3.4,1.5) -- (4.1,1.5);
  \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (3.4,-1.5) -- (4.1,-1.5);
  % axes last, so they stay visible where they pass through the tube
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-1.5,-1.05) node[below left] {$y$};
  \draw[->, thick] (-2.2,0) -- (4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \node[below] at (1.0,-2.2) {$y^2+z^2=4$};
\end{scope}
% ------------- right: the piece with 0 <= x <= 5 -------------
\begin{scope}[xshift=8.2cm]
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-1.5,-1.05) node[below left] {$y$};
  \draw[->, thick] (-1.3,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \fill[purple!20, opacity=0.8] (0,-1.5) rectangle (3.6,1.5);
  % both rim disks lighter than the wall: the tube is open, not capped,
  % so only the side wall gets the solid body color
  \fill[purple!8, opacity=0.9] (0,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \fill[purple!8, opacity=0.9] (3.6,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[purple!60!black, thick] (0,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[purple!60!black, thick] (3.6,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 1.5cm);
  \draw[purple!60!black] (0,1.5) -- (3.6,1.5);
  \draw[purple!60!black] (0,-1.5) -- (3.6,-1.5);
  \node[purple!60!black, above] at (0,1.6) {$x=0$};
  \node[purple!60!black, above] at (3.6,1.6) {$x=5$};
  \node[below] at (1.8,-2.2) {$y^2+z^2=4,\; 0\le x\le 5$};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>G.</alert> The condition <m>x = y</m> says nothing about
<m>z</m>, so whenever a point satisfies it, so does the whole vertical
line through that point. In the <m>xy</m>-plane the equation
<m>x = y</m> describes a line through the origin, and sweeping that line
vertically gives a plane: the plane containing the <m>z</m>-axis that
cuts the <m>xy</m>-plane along the line <m>y = x</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-plane-xy"/>. It is <em>not</em> parallel to any
coordinate plane, unlike part <alert>A</alert>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-plane-xy">
<caption>
Part <alert>G</alert>: the plane <m>x = y</m>. It contains the
<m>z</m>-axis and meets the <m>xy</m>-plane in the line <m>y = x</m>,
drawn in red.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded plane standing vertically and cutting diagonally through the
first and third octants. It contains the z axis, and the red line where
it meets the horizontal x y plane runs diagonally between the x and y
axes.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.95, >=stealth]
% the plane x = y is spanned by the directions (1,1,0) and (0,0,1)
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.7]
  (-2.0,-2.0,-1.4) -- (3.6,3.6,-1.4) -- (3.6,3.6,1.9) -- (-2.0,-2.0,1.9) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black]
  (-2.0,-2.0,-1.4) -- (3.6,3.6,-1.4) -- (3.6,3.6,1.9) -- (-2.0,-2.0,1.9) -- cycle;
% axes, negative parts drawn thin so the plane reads as passing through the origin
\draw[thick] (-1.8,0,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-1.4,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,4.2,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0,-1.7) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.4) node[above] {$z$};
% the trace in the xy-plane
\draw[red!70!black, very thick] (-2.0,-2.0,0) -- (3.6,3.6,0);
\node[red!70!black, below right=1pt] at (3.6,3.6,0) {$y=x$};
\node[cyan!70!black] at (0.9,0.9,1.55) {$x=y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-cylinder-plane-solid">
<title>A Solid Bounded by a Cylinder and a Plane</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the solid region bounded by the three surfaces
<md>
z = 0, \qquad x^2+y^2 = 4, \qquad x+y+z = 4,
</md>
restricted to <m>z \ge 0</m>. Describe each of the three bounding
surfaces, and describe the solid region they enclose.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The equation <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> is missing <m>z</m>, so it is the
infinite cylinder of radius <m>2</m> whose axis is the <m>z</m>-axis.
The equation <m>z=0</m> is the <m>xy</m>-plane, and <m>x+y+z=4</m> is a
plane tilted with respect to all three coordinate axes.
</p>
<p>
Solving the third equation for <m>z</m> gives <m>z = 4-x-y</m>. Over
the disk <m>x^2+y^2 \le 4</m> enclosed by the cylinder, this height
stays positive: the largest value of <m>x+y</m> on that disk is
<m>2\sqrt2 \approx 2.83</m>, attained in the direction <m>(1,1)</m>, so
<m>z = 4-x-y \ge 4-2\sqrt2 &gt; 0</m> throughout the disk. The tilted
plane therefore never dips below the <m>xy</m>-plane over the region
enclosed by the cylinder.
</p>
<p>
The solid is the piece of the solid cylinder <m>x^2+y^2 \le 4</m>
lying between the flat base <m>z=0</m> and the slanted lid
<m>z=4-x-y</m>: an oblique, truncated cylinder whose base is the disk
of radius <m>2</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane and whose slanted top is an
ellipse, shown in <xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-plane-solid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-plane-solid">
<caption>
The solid bounded by <m>z=0</m>, the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=4</m>, and the
tilted plane <m>x+y+z=4</m>. The disk in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the
flat base, and the plane, hatched in pink, cuts the cylinder in an
ellipse that forms the slanted top.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A cylinder of radius two centered on the z axis, standing on a
shaded disk in the x y plane. The cylinder is sliced off at a slant by
a tilted plane, hatched in pink, that meets it in an ellipse forming
the top of the solid.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=0.65]
\tikzmath{function a(\x,\y) {return (4.0-\x-\y);};}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\final}{2.0*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\ejez}{a(0,0)}
% the equation of the circumference
\draw[white] (0,-2.5,0) -- (2.75,0,0) node[blue,midway,below,sloped] {\footnotesize$x^2 + y^2 = 4$};
% the base disk of radius 2
\draw[thick,fill=yellow,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{0.0});
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (3.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-3,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-3,0);
\draw[thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,\ejez);
% the plane x+y+z=4
\pgfmathsetmacro{\Az}{a(2.5,2.5)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\Bz}{a(-2.5,2.5)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\Cz}{a(-2.5,-2.5)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\Dz}{a(2.5,-2.5)}
\coordinate (A) at (2.5,2.5,\Az);
\coordinate (B) at (-2.5,2.5,\Bz);
\coordinate (C) at (-2.5,-2.5,\Cz);
\coordinate (D) at (2.5,-2.5,\Dz);
% the cylinder's side wall, drawn as vertical segments up to the plane
\foreach \angulo in {0,0.01,...,\final}{
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,opacity=0.5] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,4.0-\px-\py);
}
% the intersection of the cylinder and the plane (the slanted top)
\draw[blue,thick] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0-2.0*sin(\t r)-2.0*cos(\t r)});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2831853,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0-2*sin(\t r)-2.0*cos(\t r)});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2831853,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{0.0});
% the plane, hatched
\draw[white] (C) -- (B) node[red,above,sloped,midway]{\footnotesize$x + y + z = 4$};
\draw[red,dash dot] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (A);
\fill[pattern color=pink,pattern=north east lines] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (A);
\fill[pink,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0-2.0*sin(\t r) - 2.0*cos(\t r)});
% z axis, drawn last so it stays visible above the solid
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,\ejez) -- (0,0,8) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-10">
<statement>
<p>
The vectors <m>2\,\mathbf i + 3\,\mathbf j</m>,
<m>4\,\mathbf i + \mathbf j</m> and <m>5\,\mathbf i + y\,\mathbf j</m>
have their tails at the origin. Find the value of <m>y</m> that will
make their heads collinear.
</p>
<p>
Three points are <term>collinear</term> when they all lie on one
straight line. Two points always do, so the condition is a condition on
the third: it has to lie on the line already determined by the other
two.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The heads are the points <m>(2,3)</m>, <m>(4,1)</m>, and
<m>(5,y)</m>, and we want all three on a single line. Two of them are
already given, so the line is fixed: the line through the first two has
slope
<md>
m = \frac{1-3}{4-2} = -1.
</md>
For <m>(5,y)</m> to lie on that same line, the slope from
<m>(4,1)</m> to <m>(5,y)</m> must also be <m>-1</m>:
<md>
\frac{y-1}{5-4} = -1 \;\Rightarrow\; y - 1 = -1
\;\Rightarrow\; y = 0.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Equivalently, in vector language: the three heads are collinear exactly
when the displacement vectors between them are parallel. Here
<m>\langle 4,1\rangle - \langle 2,3\rangle = \langle 2,-2\rangle</m>
and
<m>\langle 5,y\rangle - \langle 4,1\rangle = \langle 1,y-1\rangle</m>,
and these are parallel exactly when
<m>\langle 1, y-1\rangle = \tfrac12\langle 2,-2\rangle</m>, that is
<m>y - 1 = -1</m>, giving <m>y = 0</m> again.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-1a">
<statement>
<p>
Two vectors <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> are drawn with a common
tail; <m>\vec u</m> points up and to the right, <m>\vec v</m> points up
and to the left, and the angle between them is slightly larger than a
right angle. Sketch and clearly label a diagram showing <m>\vec u</m>,
<m>\vec v</m>, and <m>\vec v - \vec u</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
To make the picture concrete let us take
<m>\vec u = \left\langle 4, 2 \right\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec v = \left\langle -3, 5 \right\rangle</m>, which have roughly
the directions described. Everything below is a statement about the
<em>geometry</em>, so the same picture is correct for any pair of
vectors in that configuration.
</p>
<p>
The difference <m>\vec v - \vec u</m> is the vector that, added to
<m>\vec u</m>, gives <m>\vec v</m>. So when
<m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> are drawn from a common tail,
<m>\vec v - \vec u</m> is the arrow that runs
<em>from the tip of</em> <m>\vec u</m> <em>to the tip of</em>
<m>\vec v</m>. (Here it is
<m>\left\langle -7, 3 \right\rangle</m>.) It is easy to get this
backwards; the check is that
<m>\vec u + (\vec v - \vec u) = \vec v</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-m2b-1a"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2b-1a">
<caption>
<m>\vec v-\vec u</m> runs from the tip of <m>\vec u</m> to the tip of
<m>\vec v</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A blue arrow u points up and to the right and a green arrow v points
up and to the left from a common tail. An orange arrow runs from the
tip of u to the tip of v, representing v minus u.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-m2b-1a">
<diagram dimensions="(300,230)" margins="16">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.0,-0.8,5.0,6.0)">
<vector at="pa-A" v="(4,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pa-B" v="(-3,5)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pa-D" v="(-7,3)" tail="(4,2)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<label at="pa-Al" anchor="(4.1,1.9)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\vec u</m>
</label>
<label at="pa-Bl" anchor="(-3.1,5.1)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>\vec v</m>
</label>
<label at="pa-Dl" anchor="(0.5,3.9)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>\vec v-\vec u</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Vectors u and v drawn from a common tail, with the vector v minus u drawn from the tip of u to the tip of v.">
  <annotation ref="pa-A" text="The vector u."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-B" text="The vector v."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-D" text="The vector v minus u, drawn from the tip of u to the tip of v."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-11">
<statement>
<p>
Find the projection of
<m>-\mathbf i + 5\,\mathbf j - 2\,\mathbf k</m> on
<m>3\,\mathbf i - \mathbf j</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
With <m>\vec u = \langle -1, 5, -2\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec v = \langle 3, -1, 0\rangle</m>,
<md>
\vec u \cdot \vec v = -3 - 5 + 0 = -8,
\qquad
\|\vec v\|^2 = 9 + 1 + 0 = 10,
</md>
so
<md>
<mrow>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u
\amp = \left(\frac{\vec u \cdot \vec v}{\|\vec v\|^2}\right)\vec v
= -\frac{8}{10}\langle 3, -1, 0\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle -\frac{12}{5}, \frac45, 0\right\rangle
= -\frac{12}{5}\,\mathbf i + \frac45\,\mathbf j.</mrow>
</md>
The negative scalar tells us the projection points opposite to
<m>\vec v</m>, i.e. the angle between <m>\vec u</m> and
<m>\vec v</m> is obtuse.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2a-1">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\vec v</m> be the vector from <m>(-4,3,-1)</m> to
<m>(-2,-1,3)</m>.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Find a non-zero unit vector that is orthogonal to <m>\vec v</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the angle between <m>\vec v</m> and
<m>\vec w = 3\mathbf i - \mathbf k</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First,
<md>
<mrow>\vec v \amp = \left\langle -2-(-4),\ -1-3,\ 3-(-1) \right\rangle
= \left\langle 2,\ -4,\ 4 \right\rangle ,</mrow>
<mrow>\|\vec v\| \amp = \sqrt{4+16+16} = \sqrt{36} = 6 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> We need any non-zero <m>\vec u</m> with
<m>\vec u \cdot \vec v = 0</m>. Writing
<m>\vec u = \left\langle a,b,c\right\rangle</m>, the condition is
<md>
2a - 4b + 4c = 0 .
</md>
This is one equation in three unknowns, so there are infinitely many
answers; we just pick a convenient one. Taking <m>c = 0</m> gives
<m>2a = 4b</m>, so <m>a = 2</m>, <m>b = 1</m> works:
<md>
\vec u = \left\langle 2,\ 1,\ 0 \right\rangle ,
\qquad \vec u \cdot \vec v = 4 - 4 + 0 = 0 . 
</md>
Finally we normalize:
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\vec u}{\|\vec u\|}
       = \frac{1}{\sqrt5}\left\langle 2,\ 1,\ 0 \right\rangle
       = \left\langle \frac{2}{\sqrt5},\ \frac{1}{\sqrt5},\ 0
         \right\rangle .
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> With
<m>\vec w = \left\langle 3, 0, -1 \right\rangle</m> we have
<md>
<mrow>\vec v \cdot \vec w \amp = (2)(3) + (-4)(0) + (4)(-1) = 6 - 4 = 2 ,</mrow>
<mrow>\|\vec w\| \amp = \sqrt{9+0+1} = \sqrt{10} .</mrow>
</md>
Therefore
<md>
\cos\theta = \frac{\vec v \cdot \vec w}{\|\vec v\|\,\|\vec w\|}
           = \frac{2}{6\sqrt{10}} = \frac{1}{3\sqrt{10}}
           \approx 0.1054 ,
</md>
so
<md>
\theta = \cos^{-1}\!\left(\frac{1}{3\sqrt{10}}\right)
       \approx 1.465 \text{ radians} \approx 83.9^{\circ} .
</md>
The angle is slightly less than a right angle, which matches the fact
that the dot product is positive but small.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2a-3">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>A = (0,0,1)</m> and <m>B = (0,2,3)</m>. Find an equation for the
set of all points <m>P = (x,y,z)</m> such that <m>\vec{AP}</m> is
orthogonal to <m>\vec{BP}</m>. Then sketch the resulting surface in
three dimensions.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The two vectors are
<md>
<mrow>\vec{AP} \amp = \left\langle x-0,\ y-0,\ z-1 \right\rangle
= \left\langle x,\ y,\ z-1 \right\rangle ,</mrow>
<mrow>\vec{BP} \amp = \left\langle x,\ y-2,\ z-3 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Orthogonality means the dot product vanishes:
<md>
<mrow>\vec{AP}\cdot\vec{BP} \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>x^2 + y(y-2) + (z-1)(z-3) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>x^2 + y^2 - 2y + z^2 - 4z + 3 \amp = 0 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now complete the square in <m>y</m> and in <m>z</m>:
<md>
<mrow>x^2 + \left(y^2 - 2y + 1\right) + \left(z^2 - 4z + 4\right)
  \amp = -3 + 1 + 4</mrow>
<mrow>x^2 + (y-1)^2 + (z-2)^2 \amp = 2 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
This is the sphere of radius <m>\sqrt2</m> centred at
<m>(0,1,2)</m>. That centre is exactly the midpoint of <m>A</m> and
<m>B</m>, and the radius is exactly half of
<m>\|\vec{AB}\| = \|\langle 0,2,2\rangle\| = 2\sqrt2</m>. This is the
three-dimensional version of the familiar fact from plane geometry: the
points from which a given segment subtends a right angle form the
circle having that segment as a diameter. See
<xref ref="fig-m2a-sphere"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2a-sphere">
<caption>
The set of points <m>P</m> with <m>\vec{AP} \perp \vec{BP}</m> is the
sphere having <m>\overline{AB}</m> as a diameter, namely
<m>x^2+(y-1)^2+(z-2)^2 = 2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
A sphere in three dimensional space with the segment from A at zero,
zero, one to B at zero, two, three drawn as a diameter. A point P is
marked on the sphere and the two segments from P to A and from P to B
meet at a right angle.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}]
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (-1.6,0,0) -- (2.2,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1.2,0) -- (0,4.0,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,-0.8) -- (0,0,4.6) node[above] {$z$};
% the sphere: centre (0,1,2), radius sqrt(2)
\shade[ball color=mblue, opacity=0.30] (0,1,2) circle (1.4142cm);
% the diameter AB
\coordinate (A) at (0,0,1);
\coordinate (B) at (0,2,3);
\coordinate (P) at (0,2.1,1.15);
\draw[very thick, mred] (A) -- (B);
\draw[very thick, mgreen] (A) -- (P);
\draw[very thick, morange] (B) -- (P);
\fill[mred] (A) circle (1.7pt);
\fill[mred] (B) circle (1.7pt);
\fill[black] (0,1,2) circle (1.4pt);
\fill[mverm] (P) circle (1.7pt);
\node[mred, anchor=east] at (A) {$A(0,0,1)$\;};
\node[mred, anchor=south] at (B) {$B(0,2,3)$};
\node[anchor=south east] at (0,1,2) {$(0,1,2)$\;};
\node[mverm, anchor=west] at (P) {\;$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-1b">
<statement>
<p>
Two vectors <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> are drawn with a common
tail; <m>\vec u</m> points up and to the right, <m>\vec v</m> points up
and to the left, and the angle between them is slightly larger than a
right angle. Sketch and clearly label a diagram showing <m>\vec u</m>,
<m>\vec v</m>, and <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec u}\vec v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
To make the picture concrete let us take
<m>\vec u = \left\langle 4, 2 \right\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec v = \left\langle -3, 5 \right\rangle</m>, which have roughly
the directions described. Everything below is a statement about
the <em>geometry</em>, so the same picture is correct for any pair of
vectors in that configuration.
</p>

<p>
The projection of <m>\vec v</m> onto <m>\vec u</m> is
the shadow that <m>\vec v</m> casts on the line through
<m>\vec u</m>, obtained by dropping a perpendicular from the tip of
<m>\vec v</m> to that line. Algebraically,
<md>
<mrow>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec u}\vec v
\amp = \left(\frac{\vec u\cdot\vec v}{\|\vec u\|^2}\right)\vec u
= \left(\frac{-12+10}{20}\right)\left\langle 4,2\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -\frac{1}{10}\left\langle 4,2\right\rangle
= \left\langle -0.4,\ -0.2 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
Because the angle between <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> is
<em>obtuse</em>, the dot product is negative and the projection points
in the direction <em>opposite</em> to <m>\vec u</m>. This is the
feature of the picture the problem is testing. See
<xref ref="fig-m2b-1b"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2b-1b">
<caption>
Since the angle between <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> is obtuse,
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec u}\vec v</m> points opposite to
<m>\vec u</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A blue arrow u points up and to the right and a green arrow v points
up and to the left from a common tail. A short dark red arrow along
the line of u points backwards, away from u, with a dashed
perpendicular from the tip of v, representing the projection of v
onto u.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-m2b-1b">
<diagram dimensions="(300,240)" margins="16">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.5,-2.0,5.5,6.0)">
<line at="pb-axis" p1="(-3.5,-1.75)" p2="(5.5,2.75)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5" dash="5 4"/>
<vector at="pb-A" v="(4,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pb-B" v="(-3,5)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pb-P" v="(-0.4,-0.2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<line at="pb-drop" p1="(-3,5)" p2="(-0.4,-0.2)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5" dash="4 3"/>
<label at="pb-Al" anchor="(4.1,1.9)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\vec u</m>
</label>
<label at="pb-Bl" anchor="(-3.1,5.1)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>\vec v</m>
</label>
<label at="pb-Pl" anchor="(-0.55,-1.35)" alignment="w" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec u}\vec v</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Vectors u and v drawn from a common tail, with the projection of v onto u drawn along the line through u, pointing opposite to u since the angle between u and v is obtuse.">
  <annotation ref="pb-A" text="The vector u."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-B" text="The vector v."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-P" text="The projection of v onto u, which points opposite to u because the angle between u and v is obtuse."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="dp-norms-geometry">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> are non-zero vectors
satisfying
<md>
\|\mathbf u + \mathbf v\| = 5, \qquad \|\mathbf u - \mathbf v\| = 13,
</md>
and
<md>
\|\mathbf u\|^2 + \|\mathbf v\|^2 = 97, \qquad \|\mathbf u\| = 7 .
</md>
Without finding the coordinates of either vector:
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Compute <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find <m>\|\mathbf v\|</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the angle <m>\theta</m> between <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Determine whether the angle is acute, right, or obtuse.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The whole problem rests on one idea: the square of a length is a dot
product, <m>\|\mathbf w\|^2 = \mathbf w\cdot\mathbf w</m>. Expanding with
the distributive property,
<md>
<mrow>\|\mathbf u + \mathbf v\|^2 \amp
= (\mathbf u + \mathbf v)\cdot(\mathbf u + \mathbf v)
= \|\mathbf u\|^2 + 2\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v + \|\mathbf v\|^2 ,</mrow>
<mrow>\|\mathbf u - \mathbf v\|^2 \amp
= (\mathbf u - \mathbf v)\cdot(\mathbf u - \mathbf v)
= \|\mathbf u\|^2 - 2\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v + \|\mathbf v\|^2 .</mrow>
</md>
These two identities are the algebraic form of the parallelogram in
<xref ref="fig-dp-parallelogram"/>: the diagonals of the parallelogram
built on <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> are
<m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m> and <m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> Subtracting the second identity from the first makes
the <m>\|\mathbf u\|^2</m> and <m>\|\mathbf v\|^2</m> terms cancel, leaving
only the dot product:
<md>
\|\mathbf u + \mathbf v\|^2 - \|\mathbf u - \mathbf v\|^2
= 4\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v .
</md>
Substituting the two given lengths,
<md>
<mrow>5^2 - 13^2 \amp = 4\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v ,</mrow>
<mrow>25 - 169 \amp = 4\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v ,</mrow>
<mrow>-144 \amp = 4\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v ,</mrow>
</md>
and therefore <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v = -36</m>.
</p>

<p>
It is worth pausing to check that the data are consistent.
<em>Adding</em> the two identities instead of subtracting gives the
parallelogram law,
<md>
\|\mathbf u + \mathbf v\|^2 + \|\mathbf u - \mathbf v\|^2
= 2\left(\|\mathbf u\|^2 + \|\mathbf v\|^2\right) ,
</md>
and indeed <m>5^2 + 13^2 = 25 + 169 = 194 = 2(97)</m>, exactly as it
should be.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> We are told <m>\|\mathbf u\| = 7</m> and
<m>\|\mathbf u\|^2 + \|\mathbf v\|^2 = 97</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>7^2 + \|\mathbf v\|^2 \amp = 97 ,</mrow>
<mrow>49 + \|\mathbf v\|^2 \amp = 97 ,</mrow>
<mrow>\|\mathbf v\|^2 \amp = 48 .</mrow>
</md>
A length is never negative, so
<m>\|\mathbf v\| = \sqrt{48} = 4\sqrt3 \approx 6.93</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> For non-zero vectors,
<m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v = \|\mathbf u\|\,\|\mathbf v\|\cos\theta</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>\cos\theta \amp
= \frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf u\|\,\|\mathbf v\|}
= \frac{-36}{(7)\left(4\sqrt3\right)}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -\frac{9}{7\sqrt3} = -\frac{3\sqrt3}{7}
\approx -0.7423 ,</mrow>
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\theta = \cos^{-1}\!\left(-\frac{3\sqrt3}{7}\right)
\approx 2.407 \text{ radians} \approx 137.9^{\circ} .
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(d)</term> Since <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v = -36 \lt 0</m> and
the lengths are positive, <m>\cos\theta \lt 0</m>. Hence
<m>\tfrac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \lt \pi</m> and the angle is
<term>obtuse</term>. Notice that we could have answered this part
immediately after part (a), without knowing either length: the
<em>sign</em> of the dot product already decides the type of angle.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-dp-parallelogram">
<caption>
The parallelogram built on <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>. Its
diagonals are <m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m>, of length <m>5</m>, and
<m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>, of length <m>13</m>. One short diagonal
together with one long one forces the angle <m>\theta</m> between the
sides to be obtuse.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<description>
<p>
A parallelogram with two vectors u and v drawn from a common corner,
showing the short diagonal u plus v and the long diagonal u minus v,
with an obtuse angle marked between u and v.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-dp-parallelogram">
<diagram dimensions="(640,270)" margins="18">
<coordinates bbox="(-6.8,-1.8,13.8,6.8)">
<line at="dpp-side1" p1="(7,0)" p2="(1.857,4.643)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="5 4"/>
<line at="dpp-side2" p1="(-5.143,4.643)" p2="(1.857,4.643)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="5 4"/>
<vector at="dpp-u" v="(7,0)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpp-v" v="(-5.143,4.643)" tail="(0,0)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpp-sum" v="(1.857,4.643)" tail="(0,0)"
        stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpp-diff" v="(12.143,-4.643)" tail="(-5.143,4.643)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<angle-marker at="dpp-theta" points="((7,0),(0,0),(-5.143,4.643))"
              radius="26" clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
<label at="dpp-ul" anchor="(3.5,-0.25)" alignment="s" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf u,\ \|\mathbf u\| = 7</m>
</label>
<label at="dpp-vl" anchor="(-3.1,2.55)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>\mathbf v,\ \|\mathbf v\| = 4\sqrt3</m>
</label>
<label at="dpp-suml" anchor="(2.0,4.82)" alignment="ne" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\mathbf u+\mathbf v,\ \text{length } 5</m>
</label>
<label at="dpp-diffl" anchor="(6.2,1.55)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>\mathbf u-\mathbf v,\ \text{length } 13</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A parallelogram drawn with two solid vectors from a common corner and two dashed sides completing it. The blue vector u points to the right, the green vector v points up and to the left at an obtuse angle to u, a short dark red diagonal runs from the common corner to the opposite corner, and a long orange diagonal runs from the tip of v to the tip of u.">
  <annotation ref="dpp-u" text="The vector u, of length seven, drawn to the right from the common corner."/>
  <annotation ref="dpp-v" text="The vector v, of length four root three, drawn up and to the left, making an obtuse angle with u."/>
  <annotation ref="dpp-sum" text="The short diagonal, u plus v, of length five, running from the common corner to the far corner of the parallelogram."/>
  <annotation ref="dpp-diff" text="The long diagonal, u minus v, of length thirteen, running from the tip of v to the tip of u."/>
  <annotation ref="dpp-theta" text="The angle theta between u and v, about one hundred thirty eight degrees."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<term>A remark on the data.</term> The two diagonal lengths by
themselves already determine <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v</m>, and the
condition <m>\|\mathbf u\|^2 + \|\mathbf v\|^2 = 97</m> then follows
automatically from the parallelogram law. So the individual lengths are
<em>not</em> pinned down by that data alone: the pair
<m>\|\mathbf u\| = 4\sqrt3</m>, <m>\|\mathbf v\| = 7</m> fits equally well
and gives the same angle, while other splits of <m>97</m> give
different angles. This is why the extra condition
<m>\|\mathbf u\| = 7</m> is needed for parts (b) and (c).
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-13">
<statement>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the area of the triangle with vertices <m>(0,1,1)</m>,
<m>(-1,2,4)</m>, <m>(3,0,1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of the plane containing the triangle.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Take <m>A(0,1,1)</m> as the base vertex and form two edge vectors:
<md>
\vec a = \vec{AB} = \langle -1, 1, 3\rangle,
\qquad
\vec b = \vec{AC} = \langle 3, -1, 0\rangle.
</md>
Their cross product is
<md>
\vec a \times \vec b =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
3 \amp -1 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
= (0+3)\mathbf i - (0-9)\mathbf j + (1-3)\mathbf k
= \langle 3, 9, -2\rangle.
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The magnitude of the cross product is the area of the parallelogram
spanned by <m>\vec a</m> and <m>\vec b</m>, so the triangle has half
that area:
<md>
\text{Area} = \frac12\left\|\vec a \times \vec b\right\|
= \frac12\sqrt{9 + 81 + 4}
= \frac{\sqrt{94}}{2}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The cross product is normal to the plane, so the plane has the form
<m>3x + 9y - 2z = D</m>. Substituting the point <m>(0,1,1)</m> gives
<m>D = 0 + 9 - 2 = 7</m>, hence
<md>
3x + 9y - 2z = 7.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-1c">
<statement>
<p>
Two vectors <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> are drawn with a common
tail; <m>\vec u</m> points up and to the right, <m>\vec v</m> points up
and to the left, and the angle between them is slightly larger than a
right angle. Sketch and clearly label a diagram showing <m>\vec u</m>,
<m>\vec v</m>, and <m>\vec u \times \vec v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
To make the picture concrete let us take
<m>\vec u = \left\langle 4, 2 \right\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec v = \left\langle -3, 5 \right\rangle</m>, which have roughly
the directions described. Everything below is a statement about
the <em>geometry</em>, so the same picture is correct for any pair of
vectors in that configuration.
</p>

<p>
The cross product <m>\vec u\times\vec v</m> is
perpendicular to both, hence perpendicular to the page. The right-hand
rule (curl the fingers of the right hand from <m>\vec u</m> towards
<m>\vec v</m> through the smaller angle) shows that it points
<em>out of the page</em>, which we draw as a circle with a dot in it.
Indeed, treating the vectors as lying in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
<md>
\vec u\times\vec v
= \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
4 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
-3 \amp 5 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
= \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ (4)(5)-(2)(-3) \right\rangle
= 26\,\mathbf k ,
</md>
and the <m>\mathbf k</m>-component is positive. Its length,
<m>26</m>, is the area of the parallelogram spanned by <m>\vec u</m>
and <m>\vec v</m>. See <xref ref="fig-m2b-1c"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2b-1c">
<caption>
<m>\vec u\times\vec v</m> points out of the page, drawn as a circle
with a dot at its centre.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A blue arrow u points up and to the right and a green arrow v points
up and to the left from a common tail. A circle with a dot at its
centre represents u cross v, a vector pointing out of the page.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-m2b-1c">
<diagram dimensions="(300,290)" margins="16">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.0,-0.8,5.0,8.0)">
<vector at="pc-A" v="(4,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pc-B" v="(-3,5)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<circle at="pc-out" center="(-1.4,7.0)" radius="0.62"
        stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="2.5" fill="none"/>
<point at="pc-dot" p="(-1.4,7.0)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<label at="pc-Al" anchor="(4.1,1.9)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\vec u</m>
</label>
<label at="pc-Bl" anchor="(-3.1,5.1)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>\vec v</m>
</label>
<label at="pc-Xl" anchor="(-0.6,7.0)" alignment="e" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\vec u\times\vec v</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Vectors u and v drawn from a common tail. A circle with a dot at its centre, above and to the left, represents u cross v pointing out of the page.">
  <annotation ref="pc-A" text="The vector u."/>
  <annotation ref="pc-B" text="The vector v."/>
  <annotation ref="pc-out" text="A circle with a dot at its centre, the standard symbol for a vector pointing out of the page, representing u cross v."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-cross-circular-motion">
<title>*Circular Motion from a Perpendicular Force</title>

<introduction>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
<p>
Through this guided exercise, we prove that <em>a force of constant
magnitude that always points perpendicular to the velocity drives the
particle around a circle.</em> We then apply it to a charged particle in a
magnetic field.
</p>

<p>
Both of the products you have met so far appear, and they do
genuinely different jobs. Watch for them:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
the <term>dot product</term> is used, through <m>\vec F\cdot\vec v</m>, to determine whether
the speed of a particle acted upon by a perpendicular force can change,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
the <term>cross product</term> is used to determine the 
direction and magnitude of the force that keeps a charged 
particle moving in a uniform magnetic field.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ===================== PART I ===================== -->

<task xml:id="task-cm-speed">
<statement>
<p>
A particle of mass <m>m</m> moves under a force <m>\vec F</m> of constant
magnitude that is always perpendicular to its velocity <m>\vec v</m>.
Show that the particle's <em>speed</em> is constant.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Since <m>\vec F</m> is perpendicular to <m>\vec v</m>, we have
<md>
\vec F\cdot\vec v = 0\text{.}
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now consider the square of the speed, <m>v^2 = \vec v\cdot\vec v</m>. Using the
product rule for the dot product<fn>The product rule holds for the dot
product just as it does for ordinary multiplication:
<m>\dfrac{d}{dt}\left(\vec u\cdot\vec v\right)
= \dfrac{d\vec u}{dt}\cdot\vec v + \vec u\cdot\dfrac{d\vec v}{dt}</m>.
Taking <m>\vec u = \vec v</m> makes the two terms equal, which gives the
step used here,
<m>\dfrac{d}{dt}\left(\vec v\cdot\vec v\right)
= 2\,\vec v\cdot\dfrac{d\vec v}{dt}</m>.</fn> and then Newton's second law
<m>m\,\dfrac{d\vec v}{dt} = \vec F</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}\left(v^2\right) \amp = \frac{d}{dt}
   \left(\vec v\cdot\vec v\right)
   = 2\,\vec v\cdot\frac{d\vec v}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{2}{m}\,\vec v\cdot\vec F = 0\text{.}</mrow>
</md>
So <m>v^2</m> never changes, and hence neither does the speed <m>v</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>Why the dot product is the right tool here.</term> The quantity
<m>\vec F\cdot\vec v</m> is the rate at which the force does work on the
particle. A force with a forward component speeds the particle up; a force
with a backward component slows it down. Only the part of <m>\vec F</m>
lying along <m>\vec v</m> can change the speed, and the dot product is
exactly the mathematical tool that isolates that part. When it vanishes, can 
only change the direction of the velocity but not its magnitude, and so can 
only <em>turn</em> the particle, while the speed remains constant.
See <xref ref="fig-cm-dotproduct"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cm-dotproduct">
<caption>
Only the component of <m>\vec F</m> along <m>\vec v</m> can change the
speed. On the left that component is non-zero, so the particle speeds up;
on the right <m>\vec F\perp\vec v</m>, the component is zero, and the speed
cannot change.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
Two panels. In the left panel a force arrow leans forward over the
direction of motion and its component along the velocity is drawn in
green, so the dot product is non-zero and the speed changes. In the right
panel the force is perpendicular to the velocity, marked with a right
angle, so it has no component along the velocity, the dot product is zero,
and the speed stays constant.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cm-dotproduct">
      <diagram dimensions="(660,270)" margins="14">
      <definition>pathA(t) = (t, 0.09*t^2)</definition>
      <definition>pathB(t) = (t + 9, 0.09*t^2)</definition>
      <coordinates bbox="(-3.0,-2.4,13.6,4.4)">

      <!-- ============ PANEL A : force has a component along v ============ -->
      <parametric-curve at="da-path" function="pathA" domain="(-2.4,3.4)"
                        stroke="#BBBBBB" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="da-v" tail="(0,0)" v="(3.2,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="da-F" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.6712,1.9919)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <line at="da-drop" p1="(1.6712,1.9919)" p2="(1.6712,0)"
            stroke="#888888" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>
      <vector at="da-proj" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.6712,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="4"/>
      <point at="da-P" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <label font-size="18" at="da-vl" anchor="(3.3,0.02)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>\vec v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="da-Fl" anchor="(1.74,2.06)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00"><m>\vec F</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="da-projl" anchor="(0.84,-0.16)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
      <m>\text{component along }\vec v</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="da-concl" anchor="(0.5,-1.35)" alignment="c">
      <m>\vec F\cdot\vec v \ne 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{speed changes}</m>
      </label>

      <!-- ============ PANEL B : force purely perpendicular ============ -->
      <parametric-curve at="db-path" function="pathB" domain="(-2.4,3.4)"
                        stroke="#BBBBBB" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="db-v" tail="(9,0)" v="(3.2,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="db-F" tail="(9,0)" v="(0,2.6)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="db-right" points="((10,0),(9,0),(9,1))" radius="20" stroke="black"/>
      <point at="db-P" p="(9,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <label font-size="18" at="db-vl" anchor="(12.3,0.02)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>\vec v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="db-Fl" anchor="(9,2.7)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00"><m>\vec F</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="db-zero" anchor="(9.55,-0.55)" alignment="e" color="#009E73">
      <m>\text{no component along }\vec v</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="db-concl" anchor="(9.5,-1.35)" alignment="c">
      <m>\vec F\cdot\vec v = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{speed constant}</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="Two panels comparing a force that is not perpendicular to the velocity with one that is. In the left panel the force arrow leans forward and has a green component along the velocity, so the dot product is nonzero and the speed changes. In the right panel the force is straight up, perpendicular to the velocity, so it has no component along the velocity, the dot product is zero, and the speed stays constant.">
        <annotation ref="da-F" text="A force leaning forward, at an acute angle to the velocity."/>
        <annotation ref="da-proj" text="The component of that force along the velocity, drawn in green; it is nonzero, so the speed changes."/>
        <annotation ref="db-F" text="A force perpendicular to the velocity."/>
        <annotation ref="db-right" text="A right angle marker showing the force is perpendicular to the velocity, so its component along the velocity is zero."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-turn">
<statement>
<p>
During a short time interval <m>\Delta t</m>, show that the
<em>direction</em> of the velocity turns through the angle
<md>
\Delta\theta = \frac{F}{mv}\,\Delta t\text{.}
</md>
(Hint: draw the two velocity vectors, which have equal length, from a
common tail.)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
By the previous task the velocity never changes length; it only swings
around. So <m>\vec v(t)</m> and <m>\vec v(t+\Delta t)</m> are two vectors of
the <em>same</em> length <m>v</m>, separated by the small angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m> we are after. Drawn from a common tail they form an
isosceles triangle whose third side is
<m>\Delta\vec v = \vec v(t+\Delta t) - \vec v(t)</m>, as in
<xref ref="fig-cm-deltav"/>.
</p>

<p>
The tips of the two vectors both lie on a circle of radius <m>v</m>. The
straight side <m>\Delta\vec v</m> is the chord cutting off the arc of angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m> on that circle, and for a small angle a chord is very
nearly the arc it subtends:
<md>
\left\|\Delta\vec v\right\| = 2v\sin\frac{\Delta\theta}{2}
  \approx v\,\Delta\theta\text{.}
</md>
</p>

<p>
On the other hand Newton's second law over the short interval gives
<md>
\left\|\Delta\vec v\right\| \approx \frac{F}{m}\,\Delta t\text{.}
</md>
Equating the two expressions for <m>\left\|\Delta\vec v\right\|</m>,
<md>
v\,\Delta\theta = \frac{F}{m}\,\Delta t
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\Delta\theta = \frac{F}{mv}\,\Delta t\text{,}
</md>
which is what we wanted. Notice the rate of turning
<m>\Delta\theta/\Delta t = F/(mv)</m> is a <em>constant</em>, since
<m>F</m>, <m>m</m> and <m>v</m> all are: the particle swings around at a
steady angular rate.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cm-deltav">
<caption>
The two velocities have equal length <m>v</m> (dashed arc), so the triangle
is isosceles and the chord <m>\Delta\vec v</m> has length
<m>\approx v\,\Delta\theta</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
Two velocity arrows of equal length v are drawn from a common tail,
separated by a small angle delta theta, with a dashed circular arc of
radius v joining their tips to show the lengths are equal. A third arrow,
delta v, runs from the first tip to the second; its length is about v
times delta theta.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cm-deltav">
      <diagram dimensions="(470,336)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-1.4,-2.1,7.0,3.9)">

      <!-- dashed arc of radius v, showing both velocities have the SAME length -->
      <arc at="dv-arc" center="(0,0)" radius="4" range="(0,40)" degrees="yes"
           stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>

      <!-- the two velocity vectors, equal in length -->
      <vector at="dv-v1" tail="(0,0)" v="(4,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="dv-v2" tail="(0,0)" v="(3.0642,2.5712)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>

      <!-- their difference, which is what the force produces -->
      <vector at="dv-dv" tail="(4,0)" v="(-0.9358,2.5712)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>

      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="dv-ang" points="((1,0),(0,0),(0.766,0.643))" radius="46"
                    stroke="black"><m>\Delta\theta</m></angle-marker>

      <point at="dv-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="dv-l1" anchor="(4.08,-0.06)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\vec v(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-l2" anchor="(3.02,2.66)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\vec v(t+\Delta t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-ldv" anchor="(3.62,1.30)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\Delta\vec v</m>
      </label>

      <!-- the two lengths, marked on the vectors themselves -->
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-len1" anchor="(2.0,-0.14)" alignment="s" color="#666666"><m>v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-len2" anchor="(1.42,1.35)" alignment="nw" color="#666666"><m>v</m></label>

      <label font-size="18" at="dv-note1" anchor="(-1.2,-1.15)" alignment="e">
      <m>\text{equal lengths} \;\Rightarrow\; \|\Delta\vec v\| \approx v\,\Delta\theta</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-note2" anchor="(-1.2,-1.80)" alignment="e">
      <m>\text{Newton:}\; \|\Delta\vec v\| = \tfrac{F}{m}\Delta t
        \;\Rightarrow\; \Delta\theta = \tfrac{F}{mv}\Delta t</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="Two velocity vectors of equal length v are drawn from a common tail, separated by a small angle delta theta. A dashed circular arc of radius v joins their tips, confirming the lengths are equal. A third arrow, delta v, runs from the tip of the first to the tip of the second. Because the triangle is isosceles and the angle is small, the length of delta v is approximately v times delta theta.">
        <annotation ref="dv-arc" text="A dashed arc of radius v joining the two arrow tips, showing both velocities have the same length."/>
        <annotation ref="dv-v1" text="The velocity at time t."/>
        <annotation ref="dv-v2" text="The velocity a short time delta t later, the same length but turned through delta theta."/>
        <annotation ref="dv-dv" text="The change in velocity, delta v, joining the two tips; its length is approximately v times delta theta."/>
        <annotation ref="dv-ang" text="The small angle delta theta through which the velocity has turned."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-radius">
<statement>
<p>
Combine <m>\Delta s = v\,\Delta t</m> with the arc-length formula
<m>\Delta s = R\,\Delta\theta</m> to prove that
<md>
R = \frac{mv^2}{F}\text{.}
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
In time <m>\Delta t</m> the particle covers the arc length
<m>\Delta s = v\,\Delta t</m>. If that arc bends through the angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m>, then the radius <m>R</m> of the arc is defined by
<m>\Delta s = R\,\Delta\theta</m>. Solving for <m>R</m> and substituting
both expressions,
<md>
R = \frac{\Delta s}{\Delta\theta}
  = \frac{v\,\Delta t}{\dfrac{F}{mv}\,\Delta t}
  = \frac{mv^2}{F}\text{.}
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-circle">
<statement>
<p>
Conclude that the trajectory is a circle.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The quantity <m>R = mv^2/F</m> is built from <m>m</m>, <m>v</m> and
<m>F</m>, every one of which is constant — <m>v</m> because of the first
task, and <m>F</m> by hypothesis. So the path bends through equal angles
over equal arc lengths, everywhere along the trajectory: its radius of
curvature is the same constant <m>R</m> at every point.
</p>

<p>
A plane curve with constant radius of curvature is a circle. Concretely,
the centre of the turn always lies a fixed distance <m>R</m> from the
particle in the direction of <m>\vec F</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cm-circle">
<caption>
The velocity is tangent to the circle and the force points straight at the
centre. Equal arcs <m>\Delta s</m> subtend equal angles
<m>\Delta\theta</m>, and the ratio of the two is the constant radius
<m>R = mv^2/F</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A circle of radius R centred at O. At a point on the circle the velocity
arrow is tangent and the force arrow points from the particle straight in
toward the centre, meeting the velocity at a right angle. Two dashed radii
mark a small angle delta theta at the centre, and the arc between them,
of length delta s equals R delta theta, is highlighted.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cm-circle">
      <diagram dimensions="(430,459)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-4.3,-4.9,4.5,4.5)">

      <!-- the circular trajectory -->
      <circle at="cc-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="3"
              stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2" fill="none"/>

      <!-- the two radii bounding the small turn -->
      <line at="cc-r1" p1="(0,0)" p2="(1.7207,2.4575)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>
      <line at="cc-r2" p1="(0,0)" p2="(0.2615,2.9886)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>
      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="cc-dtheta" points="((1.7207,2.4575),(0,0),(0.2615,2.9886))"
                    radius="38" stroke="black"><m>\Delta\theta</m></angle-marker>

      <!-- the arc actually travelled -->
      <arc at="cc-ds" center="(0,0)" radius="3" range="(55,85)" degrees="yes"
           stroke="#009E73" thickness="5"/>

      <!-- velocity (tangent) and force (toward the centre) at the particle -->
      <vector at="cc-v" tail="(1.7207,2.4575)" v="(-1.4745,1.0324)"
              stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="cc-F" tail="(1.7207,2.4575)" v="(-0.8604,-1.2288)"
              stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="cc-right"
                    points="((0.9015,3.0311),(1.7207,2.4575),(1.1471,1.6383))"
                    radius="18" stroke="black"/>

      <point at="cc-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="cc-P" p="(1.7207,2.4575)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="cc-Ol" anchor="(-0.12,-0.12)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-Rl" anchor="(0.10,1.62)" alignment="w" color="#666666"><m>R</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-vl" anchor="(0.20,3.56)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2"><m>\vec v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-Fl" anchor="(1.40,1.92)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>\vec F</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-dsl" anchor="(1.18,3.20)" alignment="ne" color="#009E73">
      <m>\Delta s = R\,\Delta\theta</m>
      </label>

      <label font-size="18" at="cc-note1" anchor="(-4.1,-3.55)" alignment="e">
      <m>\Delta s = v\,\Delta t \quad\text{and}\quad \Delta\theta = \tfrac{F}{mv}\Delta t</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-note2" anchor="(-4.1,-4.35)" alignment="e">
      <m>\Rightarrow\;\; R = \dfrac{\Delta s}{\Delta\theta} = \dfrac{mv^2}{F}
        \;\;\text{— a constant}</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="A circular trajectory with centre O and radius R. At a point on the circle the velocity arrow is drawn tangent to the circle and the force arrow points from the particle straight in toward the centre, the two meeting at a right angle. Two dashed radii mark off a small angle delta theta at the centre, and the arc of the circle between them, of length delta s equals R times delta theta, is highlighted. Combining delta s equals v delta t with delta theta equals F over m v times delta t gives R equals m v squared over F, a constant.">
        <annotation ref="cc-circle" text="The circular path of the particle."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-v" text="The velocity, tangent to the circle."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-F" text="The force, pointing from the particle straight in toward the centre, perpendicular to the velocity."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-right" text="A right angle marker between the velocity and the force."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-ds" text="The arc of length delta s travelled during the short time interval."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-dtheta" text="The angle delta theta subtended at the centre by that arc."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>

<!-- ===================== PART II ===================== -->

<task xml:id="task-cm-magnitude">
<statement>
<p>
A particle of charge <m>q</m> enters a uniform magnetic field
<m>\vec B</m> with its velocity <em>perpendicular</em> to the field. The
magnetic force is
<md>
\vec F_B = q\,\vec v\times\vec B\text{.}
</md>
Show that this force has magnitude <m>|q|\,vB</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The length of a cross product is
<m>\left\|\vec v\times\vec B\right\| = vB\sin\alpha</m>, where <m>\alpha</m>
is the angle between <m>\vec v</m> and <m>\vec B</m>. Here the velocity is
perpendicular to the field, so <m>\alpha = 90^\circ</m> and
<m>\sin\alpha = 1</m>. Therefore
<md>
\left\|\vec F_B\right\| = |q|\left\|\vec v\times\vec B\right\|
 = |q|\,vB\sin 90^\circ = |q|\,vB\text{.}
</md>
Since <m>v</m> is constant (we are about to confirm this) and <m>B</m> is
uniform, this magnitude is constant — the first hypothesis of part
<xref ref="task-cm-speed" text="local"/>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-perp">
<statement>
<p>
Explain why this force is <em>always</em> perpendicular to the velocity —
not just at the initial instant, but at every moment of the motion.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This is the defining property of the cross product: <m>\vec v\times\vec B</m>
is perpendicular to <em>both</em> factors, whatever they happen to be. So
<m>\vec F_B = q\,\vec v\times\vec B</m> is perpendicular to <m>\vec v</m>
automatically, at every instant, with no computation and no special
assumption about where the particle is or which way it is heading.
</p>

<p>
If you would like it verified rather than quoted, the dot product does it in
one line.<fn>Two facts are doing the work. First, the scalar triple product
<m>\left(\vec a\times\vec b\right)\cdot\vec c</m> equals the determinant
whose rows are <m>\vec a</m>, <m>\vec b</m> and <m>\vec c</m>. Second, a
determinant with two equal rows is zero, since interchanging those two rows
both leaves the determinant unchanged and reverses its sign.</fn> Taking
<m>\vec c = \vec v</m>,
<md>
\vec F_B\cdot\vec v
 = q\left(\vec v\times\vec B\right)\cdot\vec v
 = q\begin{vmatrix}
 v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3 \\
 B_1 \amp B_2 \amp B_3 \\
 v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3
 \end{vmatrix} = 0\text{.}
</md>
</p>

<p>
So such a
magnetic field can <em>never</em> change the speed of a charged particle,
no matter how strong it is. Here the magnetic force does no work and it solely 
changes the direction of the velocity.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-orbit">
<statement>
<p>
Deduce that the particle moves in a circle, and find the radius of that
circle.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The previous two tasks establish exactly the two hypotheses of part
<xref ref="task-cm-speed" text="local"/>: the magnetic force has constant magnitude
<m>|q|vB</m>, and it is always perpendicular to the velocity. The argument
of parts <xref ref="task-cm-speed" text="local"/>–<xref ref="task-cm-circle" text="local"/> therefore
applies verbatim, and the particle travels in a circle at constant speed.
</p>

<p>
For the radius, put <m>F = |q|vB</m> into <m>R = mv^2/F</m>:
<md>
r = \frac{mv^2}{|q|vB} = \frac{mv}{|q|B}\text{.}
</md>
Equivalently, the magnetic force supplies precisely the inward force
<m>mv^2/r</m> that circular motion demands:
<md>
|q|vB = \frac{mv^2}{r}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
r = \frac{mv}{|q|B}\text{.}
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-cm-magnetic"/>. Faster particles and heavier particles
travel on wider circles; stronger fields and larger charges wind them
tighter.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cm-magnetic">
<caption>
A positive charge in a uniform field <m>\vec B</m> directed into the page.
The force <m>q\,\vec v\times\vec B</m> is perpendicular to <m>\vec v</m>
automatically, so it never changes the speed — it only bends the path into
a circle of radius <m>r = mv/(|q|B)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A uniform magnetic field into the page, shown as a grid of small crosses.
A positive charge travels counter-clockwise around a circle of radius r
centred at O. At the rightmost point of the circle its velocity points
straight up, tangent to the circle, and the magnetic force q v cross B
points straight in toward the centre, at a right angle to the velocity.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cm-magnetic">
      <diagram dimensions="(460,489)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-5.6,4.8,4.4)">

      <!-- uniform magnetic field INTO the page -->
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b00" anchor="(-3.7,-3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b10" anchor="(-1.85,-3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b20" anchor="(0,-3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b30" anchor="(1.85,-3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b40" anchor="(3.7,-3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b01" anchor="(-3.7,-1.25)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b11" anchor="(-1.85,-1.25)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b21" anchor="(0,-1.25)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b41" anchor="(3.7,-1.25)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b02" anchor="(-3.7,1.25)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b12" anchor="(-1.85,1.25)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b22" anchor="(0,1.25)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b42" anchor="(3.7,1.25)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b03" anchor="(-3.7,3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b13" anchor="(-1.85,3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b23" anchor="(0,3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b33" anchor="(1.85,3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b43" anchor="(3.7,3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>

      <!-- the circular orbit, with an arrowhead showing the sense of circulation -->
      <circle at="mb-orbit" center="(0,0)" radius="2.4"
              stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2" fill="none"/>
      <arc at="mb-dir" center="(0,0)" radius="2.4" range="(58,112)" degrees="yes"
           stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>

      <!-- radius to the particle -->
      <line at="mb-r" p1="(0,0)" p2="(2.4,0)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>

      <!-- velocity (tangent, counter-clockwise) and magnetic force (toward centre) -->
      <vector at="mb-v" tail="(2.4,0)" v="(0,1.75)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="mb-F" tail="(2.4,0)" v="(-1.4,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="mb-right" points="((2.4,1),(2.4,0),(1.4,0))"
                    radius="17" stroke="black"/>

      <point at="mb-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="mb-P" p="(2.4,0)" fill="black" radius="4"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="mb-Ol" anchor="(-0.12,-0.14)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-rl" anchor="(0.62,-0.16)" alignment="s" color="#666666"><m>r</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-vl" anchor="(2.52,1.78)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2"><m>\vec v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-Fl" anchor="(1.62,0.14)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00"><m>\vec F</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-ql" anchor="(2.56,-0.14)" alignment="se"><m>q>0</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-Bl" anchor="(-4.4,4.2)" alignment="e" color="#7A5AA8">
      <m>\vec B \text{ into the page}</m>
      </label>

      <label font-size="18" at="mb-note1" anchor="(-4.4,-3.95)" alignment="e">
      <m>\vec F = q\,\vec v\times\vec B \;\Rightarrow\; \|\vec F\| = |q|vB,
        \;\; \vec F\perp\vec v \text{ automatically}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-note2" anchor="(-4.4,-5.15)" alignment="e">
      <m>|q|vB = \dfrac{mv^2}{r} \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; r = \dfrac{mv}{|q|B}</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="A uniform magnetic field pointing into the page is shown as a grid of small crosses. A positive charge travels counter-clockwise around a circle of radius r centred at O. At the rightmost point of the circle the velocity arrow points straight up, tangent to the circle, and the magnetic force arrow q v cross B points straight in toward the centre, at a right angle to the velocity. Because the cross product is automatically perpendicular to the velocity, the force can only turn the particle and never change its speed; balancing the magnetic force against m v squared over r gives r equals m v over the absolute value of q times B.">
        <annotation ref="mb-orbit" text="The circular orbit of the charged particle."/>
        <annotation ref="mb-dir" text="An arrowhead on the orbit showing the particle circulates counter-clockwise."/>
        <annotation ref="mb-v" text="The velocity of the particle, tangent to the circle."/>
        <annotation ref="mb-F" text="The magnetic force q times v cross B, pointing from the particle in toward the centre."/>
        <annotation ref="mb-right" text="A right angle marker showing the magnetic force is perpendicular to the velocity."/>
        <annotation ref="mb-r" text="The radius r from the centre O out to the particle."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-velocity-direction">
  <title>*Speed and direction of a projectile</title>
  <statement>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
    <p>
      A particle is projected from <m>O</m> with speed <m>v_0</m> at an
      elevation <m>\theta</m> to the horizontal, as in
      <xref ref="fig-velocity-at-P"/>. At time <m>t</m> it is at the point
      <m>P</m>, moving with speed <m>v</m> in a direction making an angle
      <m>\phi</m> with the horizontal. Show that
      <md>
        v = \sqrt{\,v_0^2 - 2\,v_0\,g\,t\sin\theta + g^2 t^2\,}
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        \tan\phi = \frac{v_0\sin\theta - g\,t}{v_0\cos\theta}\text{.}
      </md>
    </p>
  <figure xml:id="fig-velocity-at-P">
    <caption>
      A particle projected from <m>O</m> with speed <m>v_0</m> at elevation
      <m>\theta</m>. At the point <m>P</m> reached after time <m>t</m>, the
      velocity <m>v</m> is tangent to the path and makes an angle <m>\phi</m>
      with the horizontal; <m>h</m> is the depth of <m>P</m> below the directrix.
    </caption>
    <image width="85%" xml:id="image-velocity-at-P">
      <!-- PreFigure source embedded inline -->
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-velocity-at-P">
      <diagram dimensions="(420,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 0.2*x*(8 - x)</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-1.2, -1, 9.6, 5.4)">
          <axes at="vp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
          <!-- directrix (for the corollary) -->
          <line at="vp-directrix" p1="(-0.5,4.45)" p2="(8.8,4.45)" stroke="gray" dash="4"/>
          <label anchor="(3.3,4.45)" alignment="n"><m>\text{directrix}</m></label>
          <!-- parabolic trajectory -->
          <graph at="vp-path" domain="(0,8)" function="f" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
          <!-- vertical fall from the directrix down to P -->
          <line at="vp-h" p1="(6,2.4)" p2="(6,4.45)" stroke="gray" dash="4"/>
          <label anchor="(6,3.42)" alignment="w"><m>h</m></label>
          <!-- initial velocity at O -->
          <line at="vp-v0" p1="(0,0)" p2="(1.06,1.70)" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
          <angle-marker at="vp-theta" points="((1,0),(0,0),(1.06,1.70))" radius="30" stroke="black"><m>\theta</m></angle-marker>
          <label anchor="(1.06,1.70)" alignment="nw"><m>v_0</m></label>
          <!-- velocity components triangle at P -->
          <line at="vp-vcos" p1="(6,2.4)" p2="(7.64,2.4)" stroke="gray" dash="3"/>
          <line at="vp-vsin" p1="(7.64,2.4)" p2="(7.64,1.09)" stroke="gray" dash="3"/>
          <label anchor="(6.82,2.4)" alignment="n"><m>v\cos\phi</m></label>
          <label anchor="(7.64,1.745)" alignment="e"><m>v\sin\phi</m></label>
          <!-- velocity vector at P (tangent to path) -->
          <line at="vp-v" p1="(6,2.4)" p2="(7.64,1.09)" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
          <angle-marker at="vp-phi" points="((7.64,1.09),(6,2.4),(7.64,2.4))" radius="26" stroke="black"><m>\phi</m></angle-marker>
          <label anchor="(6.82,1.745)" alignment="sw" offset="(-2,-2)"><m>v</m></label>
          <!-- key points -->
          <point at="vp-O" p="(0,0)" size="3"/>
          <point at="vp-P" p="(6,2.4)" size="3"/>
          <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
          <label anchor="(6,2.4)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,6)"><m>P</m></label>
        </coordinates>

        <annotations>
        <annotation ref="figure" text="A particle is launched from O and follows a parabolic path. At the point P reached after time t, the velocity v is tangent to the path and makes an angle phi with the horizontal. A horizontal directrix lies above the path, and h is the depth of P below it.">
          <annotation ref="vp-axes" text="Horizontal and vertical axes with origin at the launch point O."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-path" text="The parabolic trajectory of the particle, drawn in blue."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-directrix" text="The directrix, a horizontal dashed line above the trajectory."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-h" text="The vertical depth h of the point P below the directrix."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-v0" text="The initial velocity v naught at O, directed at elevation angle theta above the horizontal."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-theta" text="The angle theta between the horizontal and the initial velocity at O."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-v" text="The velocity v at P, tangent to the path, pointing down and to the right."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-phi" text="The angle phi between the horizontal and the velocity at P."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-vcos" text="The horizontal component v cosine phi of the velocity at P."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-vsin" text="The vertical component v sine phi of the velocity at P."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-O" text="The launch point O at the origin."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-P" text="The point P on the path reached after time t."/>
        </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Take the origin at <m>O</m> with horizontal and vertical axes. The
      coordinates of <m>P</m> at time <m>t</m> are
      <md>
        x = v_0\cos\theta\;t\text{,} \qquad
        y = v_0\sin\theta\;t - \tfrac{1}{2}g\,t^2\text{.}
      </md>
      The horizontal and vertical components of the velocity are the time
      derivatives of these, so
      <md>
        v\cos\phi = \frac{dx}{dt} = v_0\cos\theta\text{,} \qquad
        v\sin\phi = \frac{dy}{dt} = v_0\sin\theta - g\,t\text{.}
      </md>
      Squaring and adding eliminates <m>\phi</m>:
      <md>
        v^2 = v_0^2\cos^2\theta + \bigl(v_0\sin\theta - g\,t\bigr)^2
            = v_0^2 - 2\,v_0\,g\,t\sin\theta + g^2 t^2\text{,}
      </md>
      which gives the speed at time <m>t</m>. Dividing the vertical component
      by the horizontal component gives the direction,
      <md>
        \tan\phi = \frac{v_0\sin\theta - g\,t}{v_0\cos\theta}\text{,}
      </md>
      that is, <m>\phi = \arctan\!\left(\dfrac{v_0\sin\theta - g\,t}{v_0\cos\theta}\right)</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-directrix-freefall">
  <title>*Free fall from the directrix</title>
  <statement>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
    <p>
      The <term>directrix</term> of the parabolic path is the horizontal line
      at height <m>\dfrac{v_0^2}{2g}</m> above <m>O</m>. Using the previous
      exercise, show that the speed of the particle at <m>P</m> is equal to the
      speed it would acquire by falling freely from the level of the directrix
      down to <m>P</m>. That is, if <m>h</m> denotes the depth of <m>P</m>
      below the directrix (see <xref ref="fig-velocity-at-P"/>), then
      <m>v^2 = 2\,g\,h</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      From the previous exercise,
      <md>
        v^2 = v_0^2 - 2\,v_0\,g\,t\sin\theta + g^2 t^2
            = v_0^2 - 2g\!\left(v_0\,t\sin\theta - \tfrac{1}{2}g\,t^2\right)
            = v_0^2 - 2 g y\text{,}
      </md>
      since <m>y = v_0\,t\sin\theta - \tfrac{1}{2}g\,t^2</m> is the height of
      <m>P</m>. Writing this as
      <md>
        v^2 = 2g\!\left(\frac{v_0^2}{2g} - y\right)
      </md>
      and recognizing <m>\dfrac{v_0^2}{2g}</m> as the height of the directrix
      above <m>O</m>, the quantity <m>h = \dfrac{v_0^2}{2g} - y</m> is exactly
      the depth of <m>P</m> below the directrix. Hence <m>v^2 = 2\,g\,h</m>,
      the square of the speed gained by falling freely through the height
      <m>h</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-collision-directrix">
  <title>*A collision on the directrix</title>
  <introduction>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
    <p>
      Two objects move under gravity. The first is the projectile of the two
      previous exercises: launched from <m>O</m> with speed <m>v_0</m> at an
      elevation <m>\theta</m> to the horizontal. The second is a ball released
      <em>from rest</em> at a point <m>B</m> on the directrix, the horizontal
      line at height <m>\dfrac{v_0^2}{2g}</m> above <m>O</m>. We want the two
      to collide at the moment the projectile is at half of its maximum
      height, on the way <em>down</em>. See
      <xref ref="fig-collision-directrix"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-collision-directrix">
      <caption>
        The projectile reaches half of its maximum height <m>H</m> at the
        point <m>C</m> on the way down. The ball is released from rest at the
        point <m>B</m> vertically above <m>C</m> on the directrix, and falls
        the depth <m>h</m> to meet it.
      </caption>
      <image width="85%">
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-collision-directrix">
      <diagram dimensions="(440,290)" margins="6">
        <definition>f(x) = 0.55*x*(8 - x)</definition>
        <definition>C = (6.83, 4.4)</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-1.3, -1.2, 10.2, 11.6)">
          <axes at="cd-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
          <line at="cd-directrix" p1="(-0.6,9.9)" p2="(9.6,9.9)" stroke="gray"
                dash="4"/>
          <label anchor="(2.6,9.9)" alignment="n" color="gray">
            <m>\text{directrix } y = \frac{v_0^2}{2g}</m>
          </label>
          <graph at="cd-path" domain="(0,8)" function="f" stroke="blue"
                 thickness="2"/>
          <label anchor="(2.4,4.4)" alignment="s" color="blue">
            <m>\text{projectile}</m>
          </label>
          <line at="cd-apex-guide" p1="(0,8.8)" p2="(4,8.8)" stroke="gray"
                thickness="1" dash="3"/>
          <line at="cd-half-guide" p1="(0,4.4)" p2="(6.83,4.4)" stroke="gray"
                thickness="1" dash="3"/>
          <line at="cd-apex-drop" p1="(4,0)" p2="(4,8.8)" stroke="gray"
                thickness="1" dash="3"/>
          <label anchor="(-0.15,8.8)" alignment="w"><m>H</m></label>
          <label anchor="(-0.15,4.4)" alignment="w"><m>\frac{H}{2}</m></label>
          <point at="cd-apex" p="(4,8.8)" fill="blue"/>
          <point at="cd-B" p="(6.83,9.9)" fill="black"/>
          <point at="cd-C" p="C" fill="black"/>
          <vector at="cd-fall" v="(0,-5.5)" tail="(6.83,9.9)" stroke="#8B1A4A"
                  thickness="2"/>
          <label anchor="(6.95,7.15)" alignment="e" color="#8B1A4A">
            <m>h</m>
          </label>
          <label anchor="(7.05,10.1)" alignment="ne"><m>B</m></label>
          <label anchor="(7.05,4.3)" alignment="se"><m>C</m></label>
          <label anchor="(-0.15,-0.15)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
        </coordinates>

        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="figure" text="A parabolic trajectory launched from the origin O. A dashed horizontal line high above it is the directrix, at height v naught squared over two g. The apex of the parabola is at height H, and a dashed horizontal line at height H over two meets the descending branch at the collision point C. Directly above C, on the directrix, is the release point B, and a downward arrow of length h joins B to C.">
            <annotation ref="cd-path" text="The parabolic path of the projectile."/>
            <annotation ref="cd-directrix" text="The directrix, a horizontal line at height v naught squared over two g."/>
            <annotation ref="cd-B" text="The release point B on the directrix."/>
            <annotation ref="cd-C" text="The collision point C on the descending branch, at half the maximum height."/>
            <annotation ref="cd-fall" text="The depth h through which the ball falls from B to C."/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </introduction>

  <task xml:id="task-collision-times">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the projectile is at half of its maximum height at the two
        times
        <md>
          t_\pm = \frac{v_0\sin\theta}{g}\left(1 \pm \frac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)
        </md>
        and say which of these is on the descending branch.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The vertical motion is <m>y = v_0\sin\theta\,t - \tfrac12 g t^2</m>. It
        is greatest when <m>\dot y = v_0\sin\theta - g t = 0</m>, that is at
        <m>t = v_0\sin\theta/g</m>, which gives the maximum height
        <md>
          H = \frac{v_0^2\sin^2\theta}{2g}\text{.}
        </md>
        Setting <m>y = H/2</m> gives the quadratic equation
        <md>
          t^2 - \frac{2v_0\sin\theta}{g}\,t
          + \frac{v_0^2\sin^2\theta}{2g^2} = 0\text{,}
        </md>
        whose discriminant is <m>2v_0^2\sin^2\theta/g^2</m>. Hence
        <md>
          t_\pm = \frac{v_0\sin\theta}{g}
          \pm \frac{\sqrt2\,v_0\sin\theta}{2g}
          = \frac{v_0\sin\theta}{g}\left(1 \pm \frac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)\text{.}
        </md>
        These straddle the apex time <m>v_0\sin\theta/g</m> symmetrically, as
        they must. The descending crossing is the later one,
        <md>
          T = t_+ = \frac{\left(2+\sqrt2\right)v_0\sin\theta}{2g}\text{.}
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="task-collision-release-point">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the point <m>B</m> on the directrix from which the ball must be
        released.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Released from rest, the ball has no horizontal velocity, so it falls
        along a vertical line: <m>B</m> must be <em>directly above</em> the
        collision point <m>C</m>. The projectile's horizontal coordinate at
        time <m>T</m> is
        <md>
          x_C = v_0\cos\theta\cdot T
          = \frac{\left(2+\sqrt2\right)v_0^2\sin 2\theta}{4g}\text{,}
        </md>
        so the release point is
        <md>
          B = \left(\frac{\left(2+\sqrt2\right)v_0^2\sin 2\theta}{4g},\;
          \frac{v_0^2}{2g}\right)\text{.}
        </md>
        In terms of the range <m>R = \dfrac{v_0^2\sin 2\theta}{g}</m> this is
        <m>x_C = \frac{2+\sqrt2}{4}R \approx 0.854\,R</m><mdash/>comfortably
        past the apex at <m>R/2</m> and short of the landing point, as
        <xref ref="fig-collision-directrix"/> shows.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="task-collision-release-time">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find how long after the projectile is launched the ball must be
        released.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The depth of <m>C</m> below the directrix is
        <md>
          h = \frac{v_0^2}{2g} - \frac{H}{2}
          = \frac{v_0^2\left(2-\sin^2\theta\right)}{4g}\text{.}
        </md>
        Falling from rest through <m>h</m> takes a time <m>\tau</m> with
        <m>h = \tfrac12 g\tau^2</m>, so
        <md>
          \tau = \sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}}
          = \frac{v_0}{g}\sqrt{\frac{2-\sin^2\theta}{2}}\text{.}
        </md>
        The ball must therefore be released
        <md>
          t_{\text{rel}} = T - \tau
          = \frac{v_0}{g}\left[\frac{\left(2+\sqrt2\right)\sin\theta}{2}
          - \sqrt{\frac{2-\sin^2\theta}{2}}\right]
        </md>
        seconds after the projectile is launched.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="task-collision-equal-speeds">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that at the instant of the collision the two objects have exactly
        the same speed.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        By <xref ref="exercise-directrix-freefall"/>, a projectile at depth
        <m>h</m> below its directrix has speed
        <m>v_{\text{proj}} = \sqrt{2gh}</m>. The ball starts from rest
        <em>on</em> the directrix and has fallen the same depth <m>h</m>, so
        its speed is
        <md>
          v_{\text{ball}} = g\tau = g\sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}} = \sqrt{2gh}\text{.}
        </md>
        The two are equal. This is not a coincidence of our particular choice
        of <m>C</m>: the projectile always carries exactly the speed it
        <em>would</em> have gained by falling freely from the directrix, and
        the ball is doing precisely that fall. Wherever on the path we arrange
        the collision, the two objects meet with the same speed, here
        <md>
          v = \sqrt{2gh} = v_0\sqrt{\frac{2-\sin^2\theta}{2}}\text{.}
        </md>
        Their <em>directions</em> of course differ: the ball moves straight
        down, while the projectile moves down and forward at the angle
        <m>\phi</m> of <xref ref="exercise-velocity-direction"/>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="task-collision-positive-time">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Is <m>t_{\text{rel}}</m> always positive? Find the elevations
        <m>\theta</m> for which the ball is released after the projectile is
        launched, and explain what a negative <m>t_{\text{rel}}</m> would mean.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Requiring <m>t_{\text{rel}} \geq 0</m> means
        <md>
          \frac{\left(2+\sqrt2\right)\sin\theta}{2}
          \geq \sqrt{\frac{2-\sin^2\theta}{2}}\text{,}
        </md>
        and squaring gives
        <m>\sin^2\theta\left(4+2\sqrt2\right) \geq 2</m>, that is
        <md>
          \sin^2\theta \geq \frac{1}{2+\sqrt2} = \frac{2-\sqrt2}{2}
          \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
          \theta \geq \arcsin\sqrt{\frac{2-\sqrt2}{2}}
          \approx 32.8^\circ\text{.}
        </md>
        For flatter launches the required release time is negative: the ball
        would have to be dropped <em>before</em> the projectile is fired,
        because a shallow trajectory reaches its (low) half-height point sooner
        than the ball can fall the long way down from the directrix.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <conclusion>
    <p component="web">
      <xref ref="fig-collision-animation"/> puts the whole scenario in motion:
      the projectile rises to <m>H</m>, the ball is released from <m>B</m> at
      the time <m>t_{\text{rel}}</m>, the two meet at <m>C</m>, and the
      velocity arrows there have the same length.
    </p>
    <p component="print">
      An animation of the whole scenario <mdash/> the projectile rising to
      <m>H</m>, the ball released from <m>B</m> at the time
      <m>t_{\text{rel}}</m>, and the two meeting at <m>C</m> with velocity
      arrows of equal length <mdash/> accompanies the online version of these
      notes.
    </p>

    <!-- Web only: in print this could only ever be a dead still, so the
         whole sidebyside is dropped by publication-print.ptx. -->
    <sidebyside component="web" width="90%">
      <figure xml:id="fig-collision-animation">
        <caption>
          Animation: the projectile and the ball, released from rest on the
          directrix, collide at half of the maximum height with equal speeds.
        </caption>
        <video xml:id="video-collision-directrix"
               source="videos/projectile_collision_directrix_1080p.mp4"
               preview="videos/previews/projectile_collision_directrix_1080p.jpg"
               width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </conclusion>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-19">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the curve of intersection of the surfaces
<m>4x^2 + z^2 = 16</m> and <m>y = z</m>.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Parametrize the curve.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the projection of the curve onto the <m>xy</m>-plane, and give a
Cartesian equation for it.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term>
The first surface does not involve <m>y</m>; dividing by <m>16</m>,
<md>
\frac{x^2}{4} + \frac{z^2}{16} = 1,
</md>
which is an ellipse in the <m>xz</m>-plane with semi-axes <m>2</m>
(in <m>x</m>) and <m>4</m> (in <m>z</m>). It is parametrized by
<m>x = 2\cos(t)</m>, <m>z = 4\sin(t)</m>. The second surface simply
forces <m>y = z</m>, so
<md>
x = 2\cos(t), \qquad y = 4\sin(t), \qquad z = 4\sin(t),
\qquad 0 \le t \le 2\pi.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> Projecting onto the <m>xy</m>-plane just means forgetting
the <m>z</m>-coordinate, so the projected curve is parametrized by the
first two components alone,
<md>
x = 2\cos(t), \qquad y = 4\sin(t), \qquad 0 \le t \le 2\pi.
</md>
To turn that into a Cartesian equation, eliminate <m>t</m> with
<m>\cos^2 t + \sin^2 t = 1</m>:
<md>
\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{y}{4}\right)^2
= \cos^2 t + \sin^2 t = 1,
\qquad\text{that is}\qquad
\frac{x^2}{4} + \frac{y^2}{16} = 1 .
</md>
The shadow is an ellipse in the <m>xy</m>-plane with semi-axes <m>2</m>
in the <m>x</m>-direction and <m>4</m> in the <m>y</m>-direction. This
is the same ellipse we started from in the <m>xz</m>-plane, which is no
accident: the plane <m>y = z</m> carries the <m>z</m>-coordinate over to
the <m>y</m>-coordinate unchanged.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-6">
<statement>
<p>
Find the intersection of the line
<m>\mathbf r(t) = \langle 1, 0, 1\rangle + t\langle -1, 1, 1\rangle</m>
and the plane <m>2x - y + z = 1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The parametric equations of the line are
<md>
x(t) = 1 - t, \qquad y(t) = t, \qquad z(t) = 1 + t.
</md>
Substituting into the equation of the plane,
<md>
2x - y + z = 2(1-t) - t + (1+t) = 3 - 2t = 1
\;\Rightarrow\; t = 1,
</md>
and <m>x(1) = 0</m>, <m>y(1) = 1</m>, <m>z(1) = 2</m>. The
intersection is the point <m>(0, 1, 2)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-5">
<statement>
<p>
Find the distance between the two planes
<md>
x + 2y + z = 1
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
2x + 4y + 2z = 7.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Dividing the second equation by <m>2</m> gives
<m>x + 2y + z = \frac72</m>, so both planes have the same normal
vector <m>\vec n = \langle 1, 2, 1\rangle</m> and are therefore
<em>parallel</em>. (They are distinct, since <m>1 \ne \frac72</m>.)
</p>

<p>
Pick any point on the first plane, say <m>P(1,0,0)</m>, and any point
on the second, say <m>S\left(\frac72, 0, 0\right)</m>. Then
<m>\vec{PS} = \left\langle \frac52, 0, 0\right\rangle</m>, and the
distance between the planes is the length of the projection of
<m>\vec{PS}</m> onto <m>\vec n</m>:
<md>
d = \left|\vec{PS}\cdot\frac{\vec n}{\|\vec n\|}\right|
= \frac{\left|\frac52 \cdot 1 + 0 + 0\right|}{\sqrt{1+4+1}}
= \frac{5}{2\sqrt6}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-12">
<statement>
<p>
Find the angle between the planes <m>2x + 2y = 7</m> and
<m>y + z = -1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The angle between two planes equals the angle between their normal
vectors, <m>\vec n_1 = \langle 2, 2, 0\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec n_2 = \langle 0, 1, 1\rangle</m>:
<md>
\cos\theta
= \frac{\vec n_1 \cdot \vec n_2}{\|\vec n_1\|\,\|\vec n_2\|}
= \frac{0 + 2 + 0}{\sqrt8 \cdot \sqrt2}
= \frac{2}{4}
= \frac12,
</md>
so <m>\theta = \dfrac{\pi}{3}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-14">
<statement>
<p>
There is a line <m>L</m> parametrized by <m>x = 1 + 2t</m>,
<m>y = 3 + 4t</m>, <m>z = 5 + 6t</m>. The line passes through some
point <m>P</m> whose <m>z</m>-coordinate is <m>-7</m>. Find an
equation for the plane through <m>P</m> normal to <m>L</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First locate <m>P</m>. Setting <m>z = -7</m>,
<md>
5 + 6t = -7 \;\Rightarrow\; t = -2,
</md>
so <m>x = 1 + 2(-2) = -3</m> and <m>y = 3 + 4(-2) = -5</m>, i.e.
<m>P(-3, -5, -7)</m>.
</p>

<p>
A plane normal to <m>L</m> has the direction vector of <m>L</m> as its
normal vector, <m>\vec n = \langle 2, 4, 6\rangle</m>. Therefore the
plane is
<md>
2(x+3) + 4(y+5) + 6(z+7) = 0.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2a-4">
<statement>
<p>
A surveyor determines that the three points
<md>
A = (-1,2,16), \qquad B = (2,-2,17), \qquad C = (2,0,16)
</md>
all lie on a flat surface <m>W</m>.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Find the point of <m>W</m> that is closest to the point
<m>(4,4,-3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find parametric equations for a line that passes through
<m>(4,4,-3)</m>, never crosses <m>W</m>, and satisfies
<m>x(t) = y(t)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>Finding the plane.</term> Two vectors lying in <m>W</m> are
<md>
\vec{AB} = \left\langle 3,\ -4,\ 1 \right\rangle ,
\qquad
\vec{AC} = \left\langle 3,\ -2,\ 0 \right\rangle .
</md>
A normal vector is their cross product:
<md>
<mrow>\vec n \amp = \vec{AB}\times\vec{AC}
= \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
3 \amp -4 \amp 1 \\
3 \amp -2 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle (-4)(0)-(1)(-2),\ (1)(3)-(3)(0),\ (3)(-2)-(-4)(3)
\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle 2,\ 3,\ 6 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
Note <m>\|\vec n\| = \sqrt{4+9+36} = 7</m>, which will be convenient.
Using the point <m>A</m>, the plane is
<md>
<mrow>2(x+1) + 3(y-2) + 6(z-16) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>2x + 3y + 6z \amp = 100 .</mrow>
</md>
(Check: <m>B</m> gives <m>4-6+102 = 100</m> and <m>C</m> gives
<m>4+0+96 = 100</m>.)
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> The closest point of a plane to a given point
<m>Q = (4,4,-3)</m> is the foot of the perpendicular from <m>Q</m>,
which we reach by moving from <m>Q</m> in the direction of the normal.
So consider
<md>
\vec r(t) = Q + t\,\vec n = (4+2t,\ 4+3t,\ -3+6t)
</md>
and find the value of <m>t</m> for which this point lies on <m>W</m>:
<md>
<mrow>2(4+2t) + 3(4+3t) + 6(-3+6t) \amp = 100</mrow>
<mrow>8 + 4t + 12 + 9t - 18 + 36t \amp = 100</mrow>
<mrow>2 + 49t \amp = 100</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp = 2 .</mrow>
</md>
Therefore the closest point is
<md>
\vec r(2) = (4+4,\ 4+6,\ -3+12) = (8,\ 10,\ 9) .
</md>
(Check: <m>16 + 30 + 54 = 100</m>.) Incidentally, the
distance from <m>Q</m> to <m>W</m> is
<m>|t|\,\|\vec n\| = 2\cdot 7 = 14</m>.
See <xref ref="fig-m2a-plane"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2a-plane">
<caption>
Schematic picture of <xref ref="m2a-4"/>. From <m>Q(4,4,-3)</m> we
travel along the normal direction <m>\vec n = \langle 2,3,6\rangle</m>
until we hit <m>W</m>, arriving at <m>(8,10,9)</m>. Any line through
<m>Q</m> whose direction is perpendicular to <m>\vec n</m> stays
parallel to <m>W</m> and never meets it.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A tilted plane W drawn as a parallelogram, with the point Q below it.
A normal arrow runs from Q up to the foot of the perpendicular on the
plane, and a second line through Q is drawn parallel to the plane,
never meeting it.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
% the plane W as a parallelogram
\fill[mblue, opacity=0.20] (-3.2,0.9) -- (2.6,2.1) -- (4.4,0.9)
      -- (-1.4,-0.3) -- cycle;
\draw[mblue, thick] (-3.2,0.9) -- (2.6,2.1) -- (4.4,0.9)
      -- (-1.4,-0.3) -- cycle;
\node[mblue] at (-2.75,1.45) {$W$};
% the point Q and the foot F
\coordinate (Q) at (0.2,-2.5);
\coordinate (F) at (1.15,0.85);
\draw[->, very thick, mverm] (Q) -- (F)
      node[midway, right] {$\vec n=\langle 2,3,6\rangle$};
\fill[mred] (Q) circle (2pt);
\fill[mverm] (F) circle (2pt);
\node[mred, anchor=north] at (Q) {$Q(4,4,-3)$};
\node[mverm, anchor=south west] at (F) {$(8,10,9)$};
% the parallel line through Q
\draw[very thick, mgreen] (-3.0,-2.94) -- (3.6,-2.06);
\node[mgreen, anchor=north east] at (-1.3,-2.98)
      {$\ell:\ \vec d=\langle 6,6,-5\rangle$};
% right angle mark at F
\draw[mgray] ($(F)+(-0.20,-0.18)$) -- ($(F)+(-0.02,-0.30)$)
             -- ($(F)+(0.10,-0.12)$);
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> A line never crosses the plane <m>W</m> exactly when
it is parallel to <m>W</m> and does not lie in it. Since
<m>Q = (4,4,-3)</m> is not on <m>W</m> (we saw
<m>2(4)+3(4)+6(-3) = 2 \ne 100</m>), we only need the direction vector
<m>\vec d = \left\langle a, b, c\right\rangle</m> to be perpendicular
to the normal:
<md>
\vec d \cdot \vec n = 2a + 3b + 6c = 0 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
The extra requirement <m>x(t) = y(t)</m> forces the two lines
<m>x = 4 + at</m> and <m>y = 4 + bt</m> to agree for all <m>t</m>,
which happens exactly when <m>a = b</m>. Substituting <m>b = a</m>,
<md>
2a + 3a + 6c = 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad 5a = -6c .
</md>
Choosing <m>a = 6</m> and <m>c = -5</m> gives
<m>\vec d = \left\langle 6,\ 6,\ -5 \right\rangle</m>, and the line is
<md>
x = 4 + 6t, \qquad y = 4 + 6t, \qquad z = -3 - 5t .
</md>
Indeed <m>x(t) = y(t)</m> for every <m>t</m>, and
<m>\vec d\cdot\vec n = 12+18-30 = 0</m>, so the line is parallel to
<m>W</m> and, starting from a point off <m>W</m>, never reaches it.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exer-parabolic-antenna">
 <title>*A Parabolic Antenna</title>
   <introduction>
<p>
<em>Starred problem.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
<p>
     A parabolic antenna has the shape of a paraboloid of revolution: it is
     created by rotating part of a parabola around its axis.  By the
     reflection property, all signals arriving parallel to the axis are
     concentrated at the focus after reflecting off the dish, so that is
     where the receiver is mounted.  The axial cross-section of the dish is
     described by two measurements: the <em>diameter</em> <m>d</m> of the
     dish and its <em>depth</em> <m>h</m>.  Placing the vertex at the origin
     with the dish opening upward, the cross-section is the parabola
     <m>x^2 = 4py</m>, and the rim passes through the points
     <m>\left(\pm\tfrac{d}{2},\, h\right)</m>.
   </p>
   <p>
     Consider a dish antenna with diameter <m>d = 120</m> cm and depth
     <m>h = 20.3</m> cm, suitable for the amateur radio band at
     <m>5.76</m> GHz.
   </p>
   <figure xml:id="fig-antenna-model">
     <caption>
       Cross-section of the parabolic antenna: diameter <m>d</m>, depth
       <m>h</m>, focus <m>F(0,p)</m>, and opening angle <m>2\varphi</m>.
     </caption>
     <image xml:id="prefig-antenna-model" width="75%">
       <description>
<p>
A parabola opening upward with vertex at the origin.  The rim
         points at (minus d over 2, h) and (d over 2, h) are joined by a
         horizontal double arrow labeled d.  A vertical double arrow labeled
         h shows the depth from the rim down to the axis level.  The focus
         F(0,p) lies on the y axis above the rim, and two segments run from
         the focus to the rim points, enclosing the opening angle two phi.
</p>
</description>
       <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-antenna-model">
         <diagram dimensions="(540,260)" margins="5">
           <definition>d = 120</definition>
           <definition>h = 20.3</definition>
           <definition>p = d^2/(16*h)</definition>
           <definition>f(x) = x^2/(4*p)</definition>
           <coordinates bbox="[-80,-14,80,64]">
             <axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
             <graph at="dish" function="f" domain="(-60,60)" thickness="4"/>
             <line at="left-ray" endpoints="((0,p),(-d/2,h))" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
             <line at="right-ray" endpoints="((0,p),(d/2,h))" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
             <angle-marker at="opening-angle" points="((-d/2,h),(0,p),(d/2,h))" stroke="blue">
               <m>2\varphi</m>
             </angle-marker>
             <line at="diameter" endpoints="((-d/2+3,h),(d/2-3,h))" stroke="blue" arrows="2"/>
             <label at="label-d" anchor="(-30,h)" alignment="n"><m>d</m></label>
             <line at="depth" endpoints="((-d/2,h-2),(-d/2,2))" stroke="blue" arrows="2"/>
             <label at="label-h" anchor="(-d/2,h/2)" alignment="w"><m>h</m></label>
             <point at="left-rim" p="(-d/2,h)" fill="blue" alignment="nw">
               <m>\left(-\tfrac{d}{2},\,h\right)</m>
             </point>
             <point at="right-rim" p="(d/2,h)" fill="blue" alignment="ne">
               <m>\left(\tfrac{d}{2},\,h\right)</m>
             </point>
             <point at="focus" p="(0,p)" fill="blue" alignment="ne">
               <m>F(0,p)</m>
             </point>
           </coordinates>
           <annotations>
             <annotation ref="figure"
                 text="Cross-section of a parabolic dish antenna, showing its diameter, depth, focus, and opening angle">
               <annotation ref="dish" sonify="yes"
                   text="the cross-section of the dish, the parabola x squared equals 4 p y, for x between minus 60 and 60 centimeters"/>
               <annotation ref="left-rim"
                   text="the left rim point, with coordinates minus d over 2, comma, h"/>
               <annotation ref="right-rim"
                   text="the right rim point, with coordinates d over 2, comma, h"/>
               <annotation ref="diameter"
                   text="the diameter d of the dish, 120 centimeters, measured between the two rim points"/>
               <annotation ref="depth"
                   text="the depth h of the dish, 20.3 centimeters, measured from the rim level down to the level of the vertex"/>
               <annotation ref="focus"
                   text="the focus F at the point 0 comma p, about 44.3 centimeters above the vertex, which is above the rim of this shallow dish"/>
               <annotation ref="left-ray"
                   text="a segment from the focus to the left rim point"/>
               <annotation ref="right-ray"
                   text="a segment from the focus to the right rim point"/>
               <annotation ref="opening-angle"
                   text="the opening angle, 2 phi, between the two segments meeting at the focus, about 136 degrees"/>
             </annotation>
           </annotations>
         </diagram>
       </prefigure>
     </image>
   </figure>
</introduction>
 <task xml:id="task-antenna-focus">
   <statement>
     <p>
       Determine the optimal location for the receiver, that is, find the
       distance from the vertex of the dish to the focus.
     </p>
   </statement>
   <solution>
     <p>
       The receiver must sit at the focus <m>(0,p)</m>.  The rim point
       <m>\left(\tfrac{d}{2}, h\right) = (60,\, 20.3)</m> lies on the
       parabola <m>x^2 = 4py</m>, so
       <md>
         60^2 = 4p(20.3)
         \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
         p = \frac{3600}{81.2} \approx 44.3.
       </md>
     </p>
     <p>
       The receiver should be mounted on the axis about <m>44.3</m> cm above
       the vertex of the dish.  Note that <m>p \gt h</m> here, so the focus
       sits <em>above</em> the rim of this shallow dish.
     </p>
   </solution>
 </task>
 <task xml:id="task-antenna-quadratic">
   <statement>
     <p>
       Find the quadratic function <m>y = f(x)</m> (explicit form) that
       describes the curvature of the dish, and graph it (for example, in
       GeoGebra or Desmos).
     </p>
   </statement>
   <solution>
     <p>
       Solving <m>x^2 = 4py</m> for <m>y</m> with
       <m>4p = \frac{3600}{20.3} \approx 177.3</m> gives
       <md>
         y = \frac{1}{4p}\,x^2 = \frac{20.3}{3600}\,x^2 \approx \frac{x^2}{177.3},
         \qquad -60 \le x \le 60.
       </md>
     </p>
     <p>
       The graph captures the true curvature of the dish provided both axes
       use the same scale.
     </p>
   </solution>
 </task>
 <task xml:id="task-antenna-angle">
   <statement>
     <p>
       The <em>opening angle</em> <m>2\varphi</m> of the dish is the angle
       at which the two edges of the rim are seen from the focus.  Compute
       it.
     </p>
   </statement>
   <solution>
     <p>
       The focus <m>F(0,p)</m>, the point <m>(0,h)</m>, and the rim point
       <m>\left(\tfrac{d}{2}, h\right)</m> form a right triangle with legs
       <m>p - h</m> (vertical) and <m>\tfrac{d}{2}</m> (horizontal), and
       <m>\varphi</m> is the angle at the focus.  Hence
       <md>
         \tan\varphi = \frac{d/2}{p - h} = \frac{60}{44.3 - 20.3} = \frac{60}{24}
         \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
         \varphi \approx 68.2^\circ.
       </md>
     </p>
     <figure xml:id="fig-antenna-angle-triangle">
       <caption>
         The right triangle used to compute the half opening angle
         <m>\varphi</m>: legs <m>p-h</m> and <m>d/2</m>.
       </caption>
       <image xml:id="prefig-antenna-angle-triangle" width="75%">
         <description>
<p>
The same parabola and focus as before, with a red right triangle
           drawn from the focus: a vertical leg of length p minus h down to
           the rim level, a horizontal leg of length d over 2 out to the
           right rim point, and the segment from the focus to the rim point
           as hypotenuse.  The angle phi at the focus is marked.
</p>
</description>
         <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-antenna-angle-triangle">
           <diagram dimensions="(540,260)" margins="5">
             <definition>d = 120</definition>
             <definition>h = 20.3</definition>
             <definition>p = d^2/(16*h)</definition>
             <definition>f(x) = x^2/(4*p)</definition>
             <coordinates bbox="[-80,-14,80,64]">
               <axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
               <graph at="dish" function="f" domain="(-60,60)" thickness="4"/>
               <line at="left-ray" endpoints="((0,p),(-d/2,h))" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
               <line at="right-ray" endpoints="((0,p),(d/2,h))" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
               <line at="vertical-leg" endpoints="((0,p),(0,h))" stroke="red" thickness="3"/>
               <line at="horizontal-leg" endpoints="((0,h),(d/2,h))" stroke="red" thickness="3"/>
               <line at="square-1" endpoints="((0,h+4),(4,h+4))" stroke="red" thickness="1"/>
               <line at="square-2" endpoints="((4,h+4),(4,h))" stroke="red" thickness="1"/>
               <angle-marker at="half-angle" points="((0,h),(0,p),(d/2,h))" stroke="red">
                 <m>\varphi</m>
               </angle-marker>
               <label at="label-vleg" anchor="(-2,(p+h)/2)" alignment="w"><m>p-h</m></label>
               <label at="label-hleg" anchor="(d/4,h)" alignment="s"><m>d/2</m></label>
               <line at="depth" endpoints="((-d/2,h-2),(-d/2,2))" stroke="blue" arrows="2"/>
               <label at="label-h" anchor="(-d/2,h/2)" alignment="w"><m>h</m></label>
               <point at="left-rim" p="(-d/2,h)" fill="blue" alignment="nw">
                 <m>\left(-\tfrac{d}{2},\,h\right)</m>
               </point>
               <point at="right-rim" p="(d/2,h)" fill="blue" alignment="ne">
                 <m>\left(\tfrac{d}{2},\,h\right)</m>
               </point>
               <point at="focus" p="(0,p)" fill="blue" alignment="ne">
                 <m>F(0,p)</m>
               </point>
             </coordinates>
             <annotations>
               <annotation ref="figure"
                   text="The right triangle used to compute the half opening angle phi of the parabolic dish">
                 <annotation ref="dish" sonify="yes"
                     text="the cross-section of the dish, the parabola x squared equals 4 p y, for x between minus 60 and 60 centimeters"/>
                 <annotation ref="focus"
                     text="the focus F at the point 0 comma p, about 44.3 centimeters above the vertex"/>
                 <annotation ref="right-rim"
                     text="the right rim point, with coordinates d over 2, comma, h"/>
                 <annotation ref="vertical-leg"
                     text="the vertical leg of the right triangle, running from the focus down to rim level, with length p minus h, about 24 centimeters"/>
                 <annotation ref="horizontal-leg"
                     text="the horizontal leg, running from the axis out to the right rim point, with length d over 2, which is 60 centimeters"/>
                 <annotation ref="right-ray"
                     text="the hypotenuse, the segment from the focus to the right rim point"/>
                 <annotation ref="half-angle"
                     text="the half opening angle phi at the focus, about 68.2 degrees; its tangent is d over 2, divided by p minus h"/>
                 <annotation ref="depth"
                     text="the depth h of the dish, 20.3 centimeters"/>
               </annotation>
             </annotations>
           </diagram>
         </prefigure>
       </image>
     </figure>
     <p>
       The opening angle of the dish is <m>2\varphi \approx 136.4^\circ</m>.
     </p>
   </solution>
 </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-3">
<statement>
<p>
Identify and sketch the surface <m>4x^2 - y^2 + 2z^2 + 4 = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Dividing by <m>-4</m>, we can rewrite the equation as
<md>
x^2 - \frac{y^2}{4} + \frac{z^2}{2} = -1,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
\frac{y^2}{4} = 1 + x^2 + \frac{z^2}{2},
</md>
which forces <m>y^2 \ge 4</m>, i.e. <m>y \ge 2</m> or
<m>y \le -2</m>. Let us look at the traces:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
Intersection with the plane <m>x = c_1</m>:
<m>\dfrac{y^2}{4} - \dfrac{z^2}{2} = 1 + c_1^2</m>, a hyperbola.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Intersection with the plane <m>y = c_2</m>:
<m>x^2 + \dfrac{z^2}{2} = \dfrac{c_2^2}{4} - 1</m>; when
<m>|c_2| \gt 2</m>, we have ellipses as intersections.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Intersection with the plane <m>z = c_3</m>:
<m>\dfrac{y^2}{4} - x^2 = 1 + \dfrac{c_3^2}{2}</m>, a hyperbola.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
The surface is a <term>hyperboloid of two sheets</term> along the
<m>y</m>-axis, with vertices <m>(0,-2,0)</m> and <m>(0,2,0)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rs19-hyperboloid2"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rs19-hyperboloid2">
<caption>
The hyperboloid of two sheets
<m>\frac{y^2}{4} - x^2 - \frac{z^2}{2} = 1</m>: two bowl-shaped
sheets opening along the <m>y</m>-axis, with vertices at
<m>(0,\pm 2,0)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Two bowl shaped surfaces in a three dimensional coordinate system,
opening away from each other along the horizontal y axis. The left
sheet has its vertex at zero comma negative two comma zero and opens
to the left; the right sheet has its vertex at zero comma two comma
zero and opens to the right. Each sheet ends in an ellipse.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-2.3) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-3.9,0) -- (4.1,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0.9,0.65) -- (-1.25,-0.9) node[below left] {$x$};
  % right sheet: vertex at y=2, profile z = +-0.95*sqrt(y^2/2 - 2)
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.5, samples=31]
    (\x, {0.95*sqrt(0.5*\x*\x - 2)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.5, samples=31]
    (\x, {-0.95*sqrt(0.5*\x*\x - 2)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] (3.5,0) ellipse (0.34cm and 1.9cm);
  % left sheet
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=-3.5:-2, samples=31]
    (\x, {0.95*sqrt(0.5*\x*\x - 2)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=-3.5:-2, samples=31]
    (\x, {-0.95*sqrt(0.5*\x*\x - 2)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] (-3.5,0) ellipse (0.34cm and 1.9cm);
  % vertices
  \fill (2,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below right=1pt] {$(0,2,0)$};
  \fill (-2,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below left=1pt] {$(0,-2,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs-multiselect-quadrics">
<title>Which of These Are Hyperboloids?</title>
<statement>
<p>
None of the equations below is in standard position. Complete the square
where necessary, then select <em>every</em> equation whose surface is a
hyperboloid, of one sheet or of two.
</p>
</statement>
<choices randomize="yes">

<choice correct="yes">
<statement><p><m>x^2 + y^2 - z^2 - 2x = 0</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Correct. Completing the square gives
<m>(x-1)^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1</m>: one negative square, equal to a positive
constant, so a hyperboloid of <em>one</em> sheet.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
<statement><p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 - 4y = 0</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
No. This is <m>x^2 + (y-2)^2 + 4z^2 = 4</m> — every square is positive, so
it is an ellipsoid.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice correct="yes">
<statement><p><m>x^2 - y^2 - z^2 = 1</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Correct. <em>Two</em> negative squares against a positive constant gives a
hyperboloid of two sheets.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
<statement><p><m>x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 0</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
No — and this is the trap. It has the same signs as the first option, but
it equals <m>0</m> rather than a positive constant. That degenerates the
hyperboloid into an <em>elliptic cone</em>, the surface the two families of
hyperboloids approach asymptotically.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
<statement><p><m>z + 1 = x^2 - y^2</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
No. The word <q>hyperbolic</q> is a red herring: this is linear in
<m>z</m>, so it is a <em>hyperbolic paraboloid</em> (a saddle) with vertex
at <m>(0,0,-1)</m>, not a hyperboloid.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

</choices>
<solution>
<p>
Completing the square in the first and second,
<md>
<mrow>x^2 + y^2 - z^2 - 2x \amp = 0
   \;\Longrightarrow\; (x-1)^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1,</mrow>
<mrow>x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 - 4y \amp = 0
   \;\Longrightarrow\; x^2 + (y-2)^2 + 4z^2 = 4 .</mrow>
</md>
Reading off the signs: one negative square against <m>+1</m> is a
hyperboloid of one sheet; two negative squares against <m>+1</m> is a
hyperboloid of two sheets. So the first and third are hyperboloids. The
second is an ellipsoid, the fourth equals <m>0</m> and so is a cone, and
the fifth is linear in <m>z</m> and so is a hyperbolic paraboloid.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-parabolic-cylinder">
<title>Graphing a Parabolic Cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Sketch the graph of <m>z = 4 - x^2</m> over the rectangle
<m>-2 \le x \le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 4</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
Why is this surface called a <term>parabolic cylinder</term>?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Since the equation <m>z=4-x^2</m> does not involve <m>y</m>, every
cross-section of the surface at a fixed value of <m>y</m> is the
<em>same</em> parabola <m>z=4-x^2</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane, opening
downward from its vertex at <m>(0,4)</m>. Sliding that parabola along
the <m>y</m>-axis from <m>y=0</m> to <m>y=4</m> sweeps out the surface.
</p>
<p>
Over <m>-2\le x\le 2</m>, the parabola satisfies <m>4-x^2 \ge 0</m>,
with equality exactly at <m>x=\pm 2</m>, so the surface touches the
<m>xy</m>-plane along the two edges <m>x=\pm2</m> of the rectangular
region and rises to a maximum height of <m>4</m> along the ridge
<m>x=0</m>. Because it is generated by sliding a fixed curve along a
straight line, rather than curving in the <m>y</m> direction as well,
it is called a <term>parabolic cylinder</term>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-parabolic-cylinder"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-parabolic-cylinder">
<caption>
The parabolic cylinder <m>z=4-x^2</m> over the rectangle
<m>-2\le x\le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>. Every slice at a fixed
<m>y</m> is the same downward-opening parabola.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A curved sheet shaped like a downward opening parabola in the x z
plane, extended without change along the y direction over a rectangular
region in the x y plane, forming an arched, tunnel-like surface.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=0.85]
\pgfmathsetmacro{\step}{0.01}
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (2.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-2.5,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,4.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-0.5,0);
\draw[thick] (0,0,0.0) -- (0,0,4.0);
% the rectangular region in the xy-plane
\draw[gray,thick,fill=yellow,opacity=0.35] (2,0,0) -- (2,4,0) -- (-2,4,0) -- (-2,0,0) -- cycle;
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] (2,0,0) -- (2,4,0) -- (-2,4,0) -- (-2,0,0) -- cycle;
% the curves slicing the surface
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{0},{4.0 - \t*\t});
% the surface
\foreach \y in {0,\step,...,4.0}{
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{\y},{4.0 - \t*\t});
}
% the curves slicing the surface
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{4.0},{4.0 - \t*\t});
\node[blue,above right] at (0,2.5,4.125) {$z = 4 - x^2$};
% last part of the z axis
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,4.0) -- (0,0,4.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid">
<title>A Solid Bounded by a Cylinder and a Paraboloid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the solid bounded by the three surfaces
<md>
z = 0, \qquad z = x^2+y^2, \qquad x^2+y^2 = 4.
</md>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Describe each of the three bounding surfaces.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the solid region they enclose.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the cross-section of the solid in the plane <m>y=0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> The equation <m>z=0</m> is the <m>xy</m>-plane. The
equation <m>z=x^2+y^2</m> is a paraboloid opening upward from its
vertex at the origin. The equation <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> is missing
<m>z</m>, so it is the infinite cylinder of radius <m>2</m> whose axis
is the <m>z</m>-axis. The paraboloid meets that cylinder exactly where
<m>x^2+y^2=4</m>, at height <m>z=4</m>.
</p>
<p>
<term>(b)</term> The solid consists of the points inside the cylinder
lying between the flat base and the paraboloid:
<md>
x^2+y^2 \le 4, \qquad 0 \le z \le x^2+y^2.
</md>
Near the <m>z</m>-axis this range for <m>z</m> is almost nothing, since
<m>x^2+y^2 \approx 0</m> there, while at the rim <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> it is
the full interval <m>0 \le z \le 4</m>. The solid is therefore shaped
like a shallow bowl carved out from underneath the paraboloid: thin at
the center and rising to its full height <m>4</m> at the cylinder wall,
shown in <xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid"/>.
</p>
<p>
<term>(c)</term> Setting <m>y=0</m> restricts the solid's defining
inequalities to <m>-2\le x\le 2</m> and <m>0\le z\le x^2</m>: the
region under the parabola <m>z=x^2</m>, above the <m>x</m>-axis,
between <m>x=-2</m> and <m>x=2</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-cross-section"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-cross-section">
<caption>
The cross-section of the solid in the plane <m>y=0</m>: the region
under the parabola <m>z=x^2</m> for <m>-2\le x\le 2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="40%">
<description>
<p>
A two dimensional graph in the x z plane. A shaded region lies under
the upward opening parabola z equals x squared, above the x axis,
between x equals negative two and x equals two.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
% the region 0 <= z <= x^2 for -2 <= x <= 2
\fill[cyan!35, opacity=0.85] plot[domain=-2:2, samples=60] (\x,{\x*\x}) -- (2,0) -- (-2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick] plot[domain=-2:2, samples=60] (\x,{\x*\x});
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-2,0) -- (2,0);
\draw[->, thick] (-2.6,0) -- (2.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$z$};
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[dashed, gray] (2,0) -- (2,4);
\draw[dashed, gray] (-2,0) -- (-2,4);
\node[below] at (2,0) {$2$};
\node[below] at (-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[cyan!55!black, above right] at (1.3,2.0) {$z=x^2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid">
<caption>
The solid bounded by <m>z=0</m>, the paraboloid <m>z=x^2+y^2</m>, and
the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=4</m>. The solid fills the region under the
paraboloid, thin near the <m>z</m>-axis and reaching full height where
the paraboloid meets the cylinder wall.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A paraboloid opening upward from the origin, shown as a shaded bowl
sitting inside a cylindrical wall of radius two. The bowl's rim meets
the top of the cylinder, and a shaded disk in the x y plane forms the
flat base of the solid beneath the bowl.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{130}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=0.75]
\tikzmath{function f(\x) {return \x;};}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\zini}{0.5*sqrt(2.0)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\step}{0.01}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\zsig}{\zini+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\nextz}{\zini+0.5*\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\sig}{2.0*\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tini}{0.5*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tnext}{\tini+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tfin}{1.85*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tend}{2.5*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tNext}{\tfin+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\final}{2.0*pi}
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (3.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-2.5,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-2.5,0);
% the base disk
\draw[gray,thick,fill=yellow,opacity=0.35] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{0.0});
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (-2,0,0) -- (-2,0,4);
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (0,-2,0) -- (0,-2,4);
% the cylinder around the paraboloid (back)
\foreach \angulo in {\tini,\tnext,...,\tfin}{
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[pink,opacity=0.15] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,4.0);
}
% the paraboloid (for z = constant)
\foreach \altura in {\step,\sig,...,4.0}{
  \pgfmathparse{sqrt(\altura)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\radio}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=\tini:\tfin,smooth,variable=\t] ({\radio*cos(\t r)},{\radio*sin(\t r)},{\altura});
}
\draw[thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4.0);
% the curves bounding the solid
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},0,{\t*\t});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] (0,{\t},{\t*\t});
% the paraboloid (for z = constant)
\foreach \altura in {\step,\sig,...,4.0}{
  \pgfmathparse{sqrt(\altura)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\radio}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=\tfin:\tend,smooth,variable=\t] ({\radio*cos(\t r)},{\radio*sin(\t r)},{\altura});
}
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0});
% the cylinder out of the paraboloid (front)
\foreach \angulo in {\tfin,\tNext,...,\tend}{
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[pink,opacity=0.15] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,4.0);
}
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (2,0,0) -- (2,0,4);
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (0,2,0) -- (0,2,4);
\node[blue,left] at (1,-1.25,2.5) {$z = x^2 + y^2$};
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,4.0) -- (0,0,4.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the Interactive Problem Set (exercises-runestone-samples.ptx):
     its three Chapter 3 problems.  These use dev-schema elements. ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rs-mc-vector-identity">
<title>Which Identity Fails?</title>
<statement>
<p>
Exactly one of the following is <em>not</em> valid for all vectors
<m>\vec a, \vec b, \vec c</m> in <m>\R^3</m>. Which one?
</p>
</statement>
<choices randomize="yes">

<choice>
<statement>
<p><m>\vec a\cdot(\vec b\times\vec c) = (\vec a\times\vec b)\cdot\vec c</m></p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
This one is valid. Both sides equal the determinant with rows
<m>\vec a,\vec b,\vec c</m>, so the dot and the cross may be interchanged.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice correct="yes">
<statement>
<p><m>\vec a\times(\vec b\times\vec c) = (\vec a\times\vec b)\times\vec c</m></p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Correct — the cross product is <em>not associative</em>. Take
<m>\vec a = \vec b = \mathbf i</m> and <m>\vec c = \mathbf j</m>. Then
<md>
\mathbf i\times(\mathbf i\times\mathbf j) = \mathbf i\times\mathbf k
 = -\mathbf j,
\qquad
(\mathbf i\times\mathbf i)\times\mathbf j = \vec 0\times\mathbf j = \vec 0 .
</md>
The left side lies in the plane of <m>\vec b</m> and <m>\vec c</m>; the
right side lies in the plane of <m>\vec a</m> and <m>\vec b</m>. There is no
reason for those to agree.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
<statement>
<p><m>\vec a\times(\vec b + \vec c) = \vec a\times\vec b + \vec a\times\vec c</m></p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
This one is valid — the cross product <em>is</em> distributive over
addition. (It is only associativity that fails.)
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
<statement>
<p><m>\vec a\times(\vec b\times\vec c)
   = (\vec a\cdot\vec c)\,\vec b - (\vec a\cdot\vec b)\,\vec c</m></p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
This one is valid: it is the <q>BAC-CAB</q> triple product expansion. It
looks the most exotic of the four, which is exactly why it is worth
remembering that it is true.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

</choices>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs-fillin-skew-lines">
<title>Distance Between Skew Lines</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two lines
<md>
<mrow>L_1: \amp \quad \vec r = \langle 1,0,2\rangle
   + t\,\langle 2,-1,1\rangle,</mrow>
<mrow>L_2: \amp \quad \vec r = \langle 0,3,-1\rangle
   + s\,\langle 1,2,-1\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
They are skew. Let <m>\vec u,\vec v</m> be the direction vectors and let
<m>\vec w</m> join a point of <m>L_1</m> to a point of <m>L_2</m>.
</p>
<p>
The absolute value of the scalar triple product
<m>\left|\vec w\cdot(\vec u\times\vec v)\right|</m> equals
<var width="6"/>.
</p>
<p>
The distance between the two lines, to three decimal places, is
<var width="8"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<setup>
<var>
<condition number="5">
<feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
</condition>
<condition number="-5">
<feedback>
<p>
Nearly — the triple product itself is <m>-5</m>, but the question asks for
its <em>absolute value</em>.
</p>
</feedback>
</condition>
</var>
<var>
<condition number="0.845" tolerance="0.001">
<feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
</condition>
<condition number="5.916" tolerance="0.002">
<feedback>
<p>
That is <m>\left|\vec u\times\vec v\right| = \sqrt{35}</m>, the
<em>denominator</em>. You still need to divide the triple product by it.
</p>
</feedback>
</condition>
<condition number="0.141" tolerance="0.002">
<feedback>
<p>
You appear to have divided by <m>35</m> rather than by
<m>\sqrt{35}</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</condition>
</var>
</setup>
<hint>
<p>
The vector <m>\vec u\times\vec v</m> is perpendicular to both lines, so the
distance is the length of the projection of <m>\vec w</m> onto it:
<m>d = \left|\vec w\cdot(\vec u\times\vec v)\right| /
\left|\vec u\times\vec v\right|</m>.
</p>
</hint>
<solution>
<p>
With <m>\vec u = \langle 2,-1,1\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec v = \langle 1,2,-1\rangle</m>,
<md>
\vec u\times\vec v =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
2 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp -1
\end{vmatrix}
= \langle -1,\, 3,\, 5\rangle,
\qquad
\left|\vec u\times\vec v\right| = \sqrt{35}.
</md>
Taking <m>\vec w = \langle 0,3,-1\rangle - \langle 1,0,2\rangle
= \langle -1,3,-3\rangle</m>,
<md>
\vec w\cdot(\vec u\times\vec v) = 1 + 9 - 15 = -5,
\qquad
d = \frac{5}{\sqrt{35}} = \frac{\sqrt{35}}{7} \approx 0.845 .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs-parsons-no-work">
<title>Why a Magnetic Field Cannot Change a Particle's Speed</title>
<statement>
<p>
Arrange the steps below into a correct proof that the magnetic force does no
work, and therefore that a charged particle in a magnetic field moves at
constant speed.
</p>
</statement>
<!--
  Keep the content of each <m> on ONE source line inside a <block>.  Parsons
  blocks are line-oriented, so a newline inside the math splits the \( \)
  delimiters across two lines and MathJax leaves the LaTeX as literal text.
-->
<blocks randomize="yes">
<block order="1">
<p>
Let a particle of mass <m>m</m> and charge <m>q</m> move with velocity
<m>\vec v</m> through a magnetic field <m>\vec B</m>.
</p>
</block>
<block order="2">
<p>
The force on the particle is <m>\vec F = q\,\vec v\times\vec B</m>.
</p>
</block>
<block order="3">
<p>
By definition, a cross product is perpendicular to both of its factors, so
<m>\vec v\times\vec B</m> is perpendicular to <m>\vec v</m>.
</p>
</block>
<block order="4">
<p>
Hence <m>\vec F\cdot\vec v = q\left(\vec v\times\vec B\right)\cdot\vec v = 0</m>.
</p>
</block>
<block order="5">
<p>
The kinetic energy satisfies
<m>\dfrac{d}{dt}\left(\tfrac12 m\,\vec v\cdot\vec v\right) = m\,\vec v\cdot\dfrac{d\vec v}{dt} = \vec F\cdot\vec v</m>.
</p>
</block>
<block order="6">
<p>
Therefore <m>\dfrac{d}{dt}\left(\tfrac12 m v^2\right) = 0</m>, so <m>v</m>
is constant: the field changes the particle's direction but never its
speed.
</p>
</block>
</blocks>
</exercise>

</worksheet>